{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KXfiaYFiwhwH841DW2w1ju","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec2_statement.tex has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17474a62-7527-453d-99c4-703178c27619","uuid":"0b57bf3a-5a2d-4d0c-9cb1-cfcb2673cb76","timestamp":"2026-11-05T09:12:26.330Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec2_statement.tex","oldString":"3 becomes 47.9 million bits. Both numbers are from a script that\nimitates the definitions on formulas it draws with its random seed fixed\nat 1, \\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}; the length\ndepends on which literals a formula holds, so another formula of the same\nsize gives a nearby number, always under the bound. Nothing in the\nencoding grows exponentially.","newString":"3 becomes 47.9 million bits. Both numbers are from a script that\nimitates the definitions on formulas it draws with its random seed fixed\nat 1, \\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}. The length\ndepends on which literals a formula holds. So another formula of the same\nsize gives a nearby number, always under the bound. Nothing in the\nencoding grows exponentially.","originalFile":"% The verified statement, read exactly -- drafted by loop0015 item 02 (2026-11-05),\n% rewritten in plain English by item 08x (2026-11-05, owner's rule of 05:30).\n% Every line number of the comparator file is at commit 4eaee875 of the clone\n% (~/dev/untrusted/openai-math, read with cat/sed -n only); every manuscript quote\n% gives the page of paper.pdf and the line of reports/scratch/eth_audit/paper.txt.\n% Every number of this section: python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py\n% (the rho(n)/n table and the crossings) and python3 -I\n% reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py (the encoding lengths).\n% The definition-by-definition reading and the theorem with its quantifiers\n% are in Appendix B (secB_appendix_technical.tex), cited from here.\n\\section{The verified statement, read exactly}\n\\label{sec:statement}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\n\\subsection{What we are reading, and the words we need}\n\\label{sec:statement-words}\n\nOpenAI's collection \\citep{OAI:The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026}\nholds, for the result it calls family 378, one machine-checked theorem.\nIt is named \\path{exact_count_and_sat}. It is stated in the family's\nchallenge file, which is 220 lines long:\n\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean} at commit\n\\texttt{4eaee875} of the collection. This section reads that\nfile and says what the theorem asserts and what it does not. Line\nnumbers are those of the file. We quote nothing from memory.\n\nA few words first, each in one sentence.\n\\emph{Lean} is a proof assistant: a program that checks every step of a\nproof and refuses a proof with a gap.\nThe \\emph{kernel} is the small core of Lean that does the final\nchecking; when we say a statement is verified, we mean the kernel\naccepted a proof of it.\nA \\emph{formula} here is a Boolean formula in conjunctive normal form: a\nlist of \\emph{clauses}, each clause an ``or'' of a few variables or\nnegated variables, and the whole formula true when every clause is\ntrue. A \\emph{$k$-CNF} is such a formula whose clauses have at most $k$\nvariables each; 3-SAT is the problem of deciding whether a 3-CNF can be\nmade true.\nA \\emph{satisfying assignment} is a choice of true or false for every\nvariable that makes the formula true; with $n$ variables there are\n$2^n$ assignments to try, and \\emph{trying all $2^n$ assignments} is the\nmethod the title names.\nThe \\emph{comparator} is OpenAI's checking tool. It takes a\n\\emph{challenge file}, which states a theorem and leaves the proof blank,\nand a \\emph{solution}, which proves it, and checks that the two state\nthe same theorem and that the proof uses only three standard axioms. An axiom is a statement accepted without proof; the three standard ones are the ones every ordinary mathematical proof in Lean uses.\nThe file we read is the challenge file. It carries the statement with\nthe placeholder \\texttt{sorry} where the proof would go (line 218). The\nsolution proves the same statement in 3,091 files\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). We rebuilt that proof ourselves and the\nkernel reported the three standard axioms and nothing else\n(Section~\\ref{sec:replay}).\n\n\\subsection{The definitions, in words}\n\\label{sec:statement-definitions}\n\nThe theorem speaks about five kinds of object. Each is defined in the\nfile before the theorem. Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-definitions} gives\nevery definition with its line numbers; here is what each one means.\n\n\\paragraph{Formulas and the count (lines 13--51).}\nA formula is a finite set of clauses over $n$ variables, and a clause is\na finite set of signed variables. The formula \\texttt{F} has width at\nmost $k$ when every clause has at most $k$ members (line 38). The number\n\\texttt{count F} is how many of the $2^n$ assignments make the formula\ntrue (line 47). Because clauses and formulas are sets, a repeated clause\ncollapses into one; the empty clause is allowed and can never be\nsatisfied; and a clause holding both a variable and its negation is\nallowed and counts as two members. The manuscript says the same thing in\nits own words: ``Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than\n\\(k\\) literals'' (p.~1, lines~44--45).\n\n\\paragraph{The input encoding (lines 53--81).}\nThe machine does not read a formula; it reads a string of bits. The\nfile fixes how a formula becomes bits. Numbers are written in unary,\nthat is, $n$ is written as $n$ ones and a zero. Each clause becomes a\nsorted list of such numbers, the formula becomes a list of clauses, and\nthe number of variables is written in front. Two things matter. First,\nthe number of variables is part of the input, so a variable used in no\nclause still counts. Second, the string is not long: its length is a polynomial in $n$ and $m$, which means it grows like a fixed power of them and not like $2^n$. A formula with $m$ clauses of width $k$ on $n$ variables takes at most about $8\\,k\\,n\\,m$ bits. In symbols, with $L$ the length of the string,\n\\[\n  L \\;\\le\\; n + 2 + m\\,\\bigl(2k(4n+1) + 4\\bigr) \\;=\\; O(k\\,n\\,m),\n\\]\nwhere $O(k\\,n\\,m)$ is read ``at most a fixed multiple of $k\\,n\\,m$''.\nOne random formula with 125 variables, 500 clauses and width 4 becomes\n$1{,}006{,}223$ bits; one with 1,000 variables, 4,000 clauses and width\n3 becomes 47.9 million bits. Both numbers are from a script that\nimitates the definitions on formulas it draws with its random seed fixed\nat 1, \\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}; the length\ndepends on which literals a formula holds, so another formula of the same\nsize gives a nearby number, always under the bound. Nothing in the\nencoding grows exponentially.\n\n\\paragraph{The machine (lines 83--128).}\nThe machine is a Turing machine, the textbook model of a computer: a finite control that reads and writes symbols on tapes, one cell at a time. This one has several tapes: a finite table of states, a finite number of tapes, and a rule that, in each state, reads\none cell of one tape and then either stops with a yes-or-no answer, or\nwrites one symbol, or moves one head by one cell. Every part of it is\nfinite. A step is one lookup in a table with three rows per state. No\npart of the machine can hide an unbounded computation inside a step.\nThe tapes and the moves are Mathlib's standard ones (Mathlib is the\ncommunity library of mathematics for Lean, at the revision the\ncollection pins, \\texttt{d13f23b7}).\n\n\\paragraph{Runs (lines 87--90).}\nA \\emph{configuration} is a snapshot: the control's current state and the contents of every tape. A \\emph{trace} of length $t$ is a record that the machine, started in one configuration, reached another in exactly $t$ successful steps. The\nstep rule is a function, so each run is determined by its input; there\nis no randomness and no guessing.\n\n\\paragraph{Space monitoring (lines 147--189).}\nBeside each configuration the file keeps, for every tape, the furthest\ncells the head has reached to the left and to the right. The\n\\emph{visited space} is the total number of cells ever visited, summed\nover the tapes. The \\emph{count word} is a number written in binary,\nleast significant bit first (line 147).\n\n\\paragraph{The exponent and the bound (lines 130--141).}\nThe theorem's running-time bound is built from one function of $n$,\nwhich the file calls \\texttt{runtimeScale} and we write $\\rhon$:\n\\[\n  \\rhon \\;=\\; \\frac{n\\,\\bigl(\\ln\\ln(n+16)\\bigr)^2}{\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}},\n\\]\nwith $\\ln$ the natural logarithm. In words: take $n$; multiply it by\nthe square of the logarithm of the logarithm of $n + 16$; divide by the\nsquare root of the logarithm of $n + 16$. The manuscript uses the same\nlogarithm: ``Throughout, log denotes the natural logarithm'' (p.~1,\nlines 48--49; again at p.~7, lines 326--327). The $+16$ keeps the double\nlogarithm defined and positive at every $n$, including $n = 0$, so the\ntheorem needs no condition on $n$. The bound itself, called\n\\texttt{runtimeBound} (lines 136--139), is\n\\[\n  C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\,\\rhon},\n\\]\nthat is, a constant $C$, times a power $d$ of the input length $L$,\ntimes two raised to $A$ times $\\rhon$. We call $\\rhon$ \\emph{the\nexponent} and $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ \\emph{the bound} throughout the\npaper. The constants $C$, $A$ and $d$ are the subject of the next\nsubsection.\n\n\\subsection{What the theorem says, and in what order}\n\\label{sec:statement-theorem}\n\nLines 194--217 state the theorem. Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-theorem}\ndisplays it with every quantifier and the line of every clause. In\nwords, for every fixed width $k$ of at least 2:\n\n\\begin{quote}\nThere exist a machine, a count tape, an answer tape, and five positive\nconstants $C, A, S, d, e$, such that for every number of variables $n$\nand every formula $F$ on $n$ variables of width at most $k$, the\nmachine started on the encoding of $F$ halts after some number of steps,\n\\texttt{time}, with the count of satisfying assignments written in\nbinary on the count tape, the bit ``is the count positive'' on the\nanswer tape and in its halting state, a count word of at most $n + 1$\nbits, \\texttt{time} at most $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, and never more\nthan $S\\,(L+1)^e$ cells visited.\n\\end{quote}\n\nThree features of the order of the words matter for everything that\nfollows.\n\n\\paragraph{The machine and the constants come after $k$ and before $n$.}\nFor each width $k$ there is one machine and one set of constants, and\nthey serve every formula of every size. They may depend on $k$ and on\nnothing else. This is what complexity theorists write as $O_k(1)$: a\nconstant that depends only on $k$.\n\n\\paragraph{There is no lower limit on $n$.}\nThe manuscript's Theorem~1.1 and its Proposition~6.4 are stated for\n$n \\ge 20$ (p.~2, lines 56--57; p.~43, lines 2474--2475). The verified\ntheorem has no such condition; the $+16$ in the exponent is what makes\nthat possible.\n\n\\paragraph{The constants exist, and that is all.}\nThe theorem says that values of $C, A, S, d, e$ exist. It names none\nof them. The proof obtains them from other existence lemmas (a lemma is a small theorem proved on the way to a larger one) and never writes a number down. We read the proof for the values it does pin:\nat the point where the main run is assembled, the exponent constant is\nset to $A = 256 + A_t$ with $A_t$ at least $1$\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 51;\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean}, lines 792--793). So\nthe proof as written has $A \\ge 257$, and the theorem that the\ncomparator exhibits adds one more\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}, $A \\ge 258$). No closed value of any of the\nfive constants exists anywhere. The machine is explicit in one respect:\nit is produced mechanically (``compiled'') from a program that uses 80 one-bit memory cells (``registers'') and 1,220 tapes\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line 11; the machine is built from\nthat program at lines 98--101). How many states it\nhas is never computed.\n\n\\subsection{The exponent, in numbers}\n\\label{sec:statement-numbers}\n\nEverything the title says rests on one comparison: the exponent $\\rhon$\nagainst $n$ itself. If $\\rhon$ is below $n$, the bound's exponential\npart $2^{\\rhon}$ is below $2^n$, the cost of trying every assignment.\nIf $\\rhon$ is above $n$, it is not.\n\nThe ratio $\\rhon / n$ depends only on $y = \\ln(n+16)$: it equals\n$(\\ln y)^2 / \\sqrt{y}$. That ratio starts at $0.64$ for $n = 1$ and\nrises. It crosses $1$ between $n = 49$ and $n = 50$: $\\rho(49)/49 =\n0.9994$ and $\\rho(50)/50 = 1.0027$. It keeps rising to a peak of\n$16/e^2 = 2.165$, reached when $\\ln y = 4$, which is at $n \\approx\n10^{23.7}$, a number with 24 digits. Then it falls, and it is back below\n$1$ only when $y = 5503.7$, which is at $n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx\n10^{2390}$, a number with 2,391 digits.\nTable~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale} gives the values and\nFigures~\\ref{fig:central} and \\ref{fig:runtime-scale} draw them. Every\nnumber is printed by \\repofile{paper3/scripts/runtime_scale.py}, run\nunder \\texttt{python3 -I}; it imports nothing from the clone and works\nin $y$, so that no $n$ beyond the range of floating-point arithmetic is\never formed.\n\nTwo facts are true at once, and the title is about the second. In the\nlimit, $\\rhon / n$ goes to $0$: the exponent grows more slowly than\n$n$, which is what ``subexponential'' means, and that is a theorem in\nour companion Lean \\leanref{rho-little-o}. And on every size anyone\nwill ever write down, from $50$ to a number with 2,391 digits, the\nexponent is above $n$.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The verified exponent compared with $n$. The second column is\nthe ratio $\\rhon/n$; where it is above $1$, the bound's exponential\npart $2^{\\rhon}$ (with $A = 1$) is above $2^n$, the cost of trying\nevery assignment. The third column is $\\rhon$ itself. $\\rhon =\nn(\\ln\\ln(n+16))^2/\\sqrt{\\ln(n+16)}$, the comparator's\n\\texttt{runtimeScale} (lines 132--134), natural logarithms. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}\n\\label{tab:runtime-scale}\n\\input{tables/runtime_scale}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n.pdf}\\\\[4pt]\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{rho_over_n_practical.pdf}\n\\caption{The verified exponent against trying every assignment. Each\ncurve is an exponent divided by $n$; the dashed line at $1$ is $2^n$,\nthe cost of trying every assignment, and a curve above the line is a\nguarantee worse than that. Top: the whole range, the horizontal axis\nbeing the number of digits of $n$, from $n = 10$ to $n = 10^{3000}$.\nThe solid curve is the verified exponent $\\rhon/n$; the two curves\nbeneath it are the manuscript's two sharper exponents,\n$n\\ln\\ln n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (the manuscript's Theorem~1.1, general case) and $n/\\sqrt{\\ln n}$ (its fixed-clause-density case), which are not in the Lean library\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}). Bottom: the same three curves over the\npractical range $n = 10$ to $10^6$, with $\\rhon/n$ marked at $n = 50,\n125, 10^3, 10^4, 10^6$. All curves take the constant $A$ to be $1$; the\nverified $A$ is unknown and at least 257 in the proof on record.\nRegenerate: \\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/rho\\_over\\_n.py}.}\n\\label{fig:central}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{runtime_scale.pdf}\n\\caption{The ratio $\\rhon/n$ alone, over an enormous range: the\nhorizontal axis is the number of digits of $n$, from $n = 10$ to\n$n = 10^{2500}$. The level $1$ is the cost of trying every assignment.\nMarked: the first whole number above the level ($n = 50$); the peak\n($16/e^2 = 2.17$, at $n \\approx 10^{23.7}$); the return below the level\n($n \\approx e^{5504} \\approx 10^{2390}$); and, shaded, the practical\nrange $50 \\le n \\le 10^6$. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/runtime\\_scale.py}.}\n\\label{fig:runtime-scale}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{The title's claim, made precise}\n\\label{sec:statement-title}\n\nThe title says the verified algorithm is slower than trying all $2^n$\nassignments at every practical size. Read against the theorem, that\nmeans the following four things, and nothing more.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{What is verified is a guarantee, not a running time.} The\n  theorem says the machine halts within $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps.\n  It does not say how many steps the machine actually takes on any\n  input. No implementation of the machine exists to be timed. A bound\n  above $2^n$ does not say the machine is slow; it says the proof\n  promises nothing better than $2^n$ there.\n\\item \\textbf{The constants are never computed.} The theorem asserts\n  that $C, A, S, d, e$ exist. The proof builds them from other\n  existence lemmas and states no value, beyond the $A \\ge 257$ read\n  above. The machine's number of states is likewise never computed.\n\\item \\textbf{With $A = 1$, the verified exponent is above $n$ for every\n  $n$ from 50 up to about $10^{2390}$.} That is\n  Table~\\ref{tab:runtime-scale}. Over that range the exponential part\n  of the bound is at least $2^n$, and at its worst it is $2^{2.17\\,n}$.\n  With the true $A$, whatever it is, the exponential part is $2^{A\\rhon}$,\n  which is at least $2^{\\rhon}$ as soon as $A \\ge 1$; and the proof's\n  own construction has $A \\ge 257$. So nothing on record puts the bound\n  below $2^n$ at any practical size.\n\\item \\textbf{Hence, at every practical size, the verified guarantee is\n  weaker than the guarantee of trying all assignments.} Trying all\n  $2^n$ assignments and checking each against the $m$ clauses counts\n  the satisfying ones in $2^n$ times a small polynomial in $n + m$\n  steps, with constants one can write down. The verified guarantee is\n  $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ with $\\rhon > n$ on $50 \\le n < e^{5504}$ and\n  $C, A, d$ unknown. For every $C \\ge 1$, $A \\ge 1$ and $d \\ge 1$ the\n  verified bound is above $2^n$ on every $n$ in that range. The only\n  values on record are the proof's own, which has $1 \\le C$ and\n  $1 \\le A$ in the statement of its main run\n  (\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 12) and\n  $A \\ge 257$ in its construction. A proof with $A < 1$ would be a\n  different proof; none is on record.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nNone of this is a defect of the formalisation or of the proof. It is\nwhat a theorem of this shape means when the constants in it are not\ntracked. The manuscript itself calls $\\rhon$ ``the weaker exponent''\n(p.~2, line~63), and its sharper exponents are not in the Lean library\n(Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}). One detail of bookkeeping: inside the\nsolution the exponent is written with base-2 logarithms, and a lemma shows the two forms differ only by a fixed multiplier, which can be folded into the constant $A$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-theorem}); nothing above depends on it.\n\n\\subsection{What the statement does establish}\n\\label{sec:statement-establishes}\n\nFor each fixed width $k \\ge 2$, there is a deterministic machine that,\ngiven any $k$-CNF on $n$ variables with $m$ clauses in the fixed\nencoding, halts with the exact number of satisfying assignments written\nin binary on a named tape, within $C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$ steps. Here\n$L$ grows no faster than a polynomial in $n + m$, and $\\rhon$ grows more\nslowly than $n$. A formula of width $k$ on $n$ variables cannot have\nmore than about $n^k$ distinct clauses ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$,\n$k = 3$; Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-theorem}), so the whole bound is\nsubexponential in $n$ alone. In the standard vocabulary, where $o(n)$ means a quantity that grows more slowly than $n$ and $\\mathrm{poly}$ a polynomial: a deterministic $2^{o(n)} \\cdot \\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ algorithm for counting the solutions\nof $k$-SAT, and therefore for deciding it. That refutes the Exponential\nTime Hypothesis \\citep{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, the conjecture that\n3-SAT needs time exponential in $n$, as stated. The verified theorem is\nthe manuscript's own verified form, the weaker exponent of its\nProposition~6.4 (p.~43, lines 2474--2476), without the $n \\ge 20$\ncondition. We found no field, definition or quantifier through which\nthe statement could be accepted by the kernel and yet mean something\nweaker than this; the audit behind that sentence is\n\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/statement_audit.md}. Our own\nrestatement of the theorem as a named Lean sentence, over a verbatim\ncopy of the machine model, is \\leanref{subexpcount}; it is never proved\nand never assumed there (Section~\\ref{sec:lean}).\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:statement-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No running time of any machine.} Nothing here measures or\n  estimates how long the verified machine, or any program, takes on any\n  input. The title compares two \\emph{guarantees}.\n  Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner} is the only place in the paper where\n  anything is timed, and it times ordinary solvers, not this machine.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement about the proof.} That the statement means\n  what we say it means is a reading of 220 lines. Whether the proof is\n  sound is Section~\\ref{sec:proof} and Section~\\ref{sec:replay}, which\n  found no flaw. The one proof-side fact quoted here, $A = 256 + A_t$,\n  is a line of the solution read for the value it assigns, not a claim\n  about the argument.\n\\item \\textbf{No value of the constants.} $A \\ge 257$ is what the proof\n  writes down, not what the machine needs; a different proof of the\n  same statement could have any $A > 0$. The statement itself fixes\n  nothing.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the manuscript's sharper exponents}, which\n  are not in the Lean library and are Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}'s\n  subject.\n\\item \\textbf{No claim about sizes beyond $e^{5504}$.} Past\n  $n \\approx 10^{2390}$ the verified exponent is below $n$ again at\n  $A = 1$; we say nothing about that range except that it is not a\n  practical one.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\par\\endgroup\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":86,"oldLines":8,"newStart":86,"newLines":8,"lines":[" $1{,}006{,}223$ bits; one with 1,000 variables, 4,000 clauses and width"," 3 becomes 47.9 million bits. Both numbers are from a script that"," imitates the definitions on formulas it draws with its random seed fixed","-at 1, \\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}; the length","-depends on which literals a formula holds, so another formula of the same","+at 1, \\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py}. The length","+depends on which literals a formula holds. So another formula of the same"," size gives a nearby number, always under the bound. Nothing in the"," encoding grows exponentially."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KXfiaYFiwhwH841DW2w1ju","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
