{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FWPFtrH1VkEk3jrbcv94fo","type":"tool_result","content":"== sec4:37,44\nThe clone of \\texttt{openai/math} at commit \\texttt{4eaee875} is treated\nas untrusted data. Every file was read with \\texttt{cat}, \\texttt{sed},\n\\texttt{grep} and \\texttt{python3 -I}, and nothing in it was built,\nimported or executed for this section. Two scripts from the audit of\n2026-11-05 do the mechanical part. \\texttt{closure.py} follows the\n\\texttt{import} lines from the solution's main module, strips comments,\nand counts how often each keyword of interest occurs in what remains.\n\\texttt{defdiff.py} compares each definition in the challenge file with\n== sec4:108,114\nThe non-zero counts are ordinary. Nine declarations are \\texttt{opaque},\na keyword that hides a definition's body from the proof-writing layer;\nevery one of the nine has a body, and the kernel checks that body\nagainst its type, so nothing is assumed. The 1,048 \\texttt{noncomputable}\ndeclarations, the eight \\texttt{open Classical} and the four explicit\nuses each of \\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{propext} are the\nclassical reasoning the comparator permits. The 148 instances and 40 local instances are standard bookkeeping declarations (``typeclass instances'') that tell Lean, for example, that a type is finite or a property is decidable; they are harmless because such a declaration can only make a false claim by way of an axiom, and there is none. The only options set in the closure switch off a check on notation in two files and have no bearing on soundness.\n== sec4:131,142\nThe solution does not import the challenge file. Instead the library\ndeclares again, under the same names, every definition the statement\nuses, spread over seven files. So one has to check that the two copies\nagree. The definition diff compares \\textbf{35} declarations:\n\\textbf{33} are identical character for character once spacing is\nignored, and the remaining two, \\texttt{runtimeScale} and\n\\texttt{runtimeBound}, differ only in that the challenge marks each\ndefinition \\texttt{noncomputable} by itself where the library marks a\nwhole section so (\\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean}, line~15), a\ndifference the kernel does not see. The theorem statement itself, from\nits first word to the start of its proof, is \\textbf{identical} in the\ntwo files, 1,353 characters raw and 1,124 after normalisation, at\n== sec4:222,228\nThe Lean version is fixed (\\clonefile{lean/lean-toolchain}:\n\\texttt{leanprover/lean4:v4.34.1}; \\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}\nline~8: \\texttt{fixedToolchain := true}). The manifest (the list of the packages the build depends on, each at an exact version) lists 42 packages; Mathlib is pinned to upstream revision\n\\texttt{d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612} at\n\\path{leanprover-community/mathlib4}, and the audit of 2026-11-05\nconfirmed that revision on GitHub as the upstream commit that moves to\n\\texttt{v4.34.1}. The repository carries 23 compatibility patches under\n== sec4:244,252\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{No saved axiom check.} \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}\n  ends with five \\texttt{\\#print axioms} commands (lines 197--201), and\n  seven other files of the closure carry fourteen more (nineteen\n  commands in eight files; the family's directory holds seventeen such\n  files, but nine of them are among the 886 never built). Their output\n  exists only while the build runs and is recorded nowhere: a search of\n  the whole clone for the string \\texttt{depends on axioms} finds\n  nothing.\n== sec4:275,283\nTwo smaller consistency facts, for the record. The manuscript's source\nis dated ``17 October 2026, revised 25 October 2026''\n(\\clonefile{preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/build/main_full_version.tex},\nline~25); the collection's history file mentions the family once, in the\nsentence that names the working group at whose request the materials\nwere released (\\clonefile{history.md}, line~18), and its only\n``Formalization Fix'' entry (line~7) concerns another family. And the\nscope note \\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md} states the verified exponent as\n$n(\\log\\log n)^2/\\sqrt{\\log n}$ (line~9), which is what the library\n== sec4:289,296\nBefore anything was run, the proof's trust rested on five things:\n(i) OpenAI's unpublished build, since nothing showed the closure\ntype-checks; (ii) the absence of any recorded axiom output; (iii) the\nLean kernel of \\texttt{v4.34.1} and Mathlib \\texttt{d13f23b7} being what\nupstream publishes; (iv) the comparator run, not reproduced; (v) no\nsecond checker for this family. Table~\\ref{tab:proof-trust} says which\nof the five Section~\\ref{sec:replay} moved.\n\n== sec5:22,32\nA fresh copy of the clone (commit \\texttt{4eaee875}) was built inside a\nsandbox on the project's machine. The sandbox tool is \\texttt{bwrap}\n(bubblewrap). Inside it, the system directories are read-only. The home\ndirectory is a private, empty one (a \\texttt{tmpfs}, a scratch file\nsystem held in memory and gone when the sandbox ends), so nothing of the\nreal home or of this repository is visible. Processes, inter-process\ncommunication and host names are kept apart from the host's (separate\n``namespaces''). The whole sandbox is confined to eight cores\n(\\texttt{taskset -c 0-7}) at the lowest scheduling priority. And the\nnetwork is on, because the repository's build file\n(its \\texttt{lakefile}) clones its 42 dependencies (the packages of its manifest, Section~\\ref{sec:proof}) at build time and the compiled Mathlib comes\n== sec5:46,52\nTwo runs were needed. The first, started at 01:03 on 2026-11-05, failed\nat once at the build step because its command line carried\n\\texttt{-j 8}, an option this version of the build tool does not have\n(the whole of its build log is the one line \\texttt{error: unknown short\noption '-j'}); nothing of the family was compiled. The second, started\nat 01:16 with that option removed and the core cap moved to\n\\texttt{taskset}, is the run reported below. Both scripts, both\n== sec5:71,78\nEight source files of the closure carry nineteen \\texttt{\\#print\naxioms} commands (seventeen files of the family's directory do, but\nnine of those are never built), so the build log records nineteen\naxiom lines as it goes. Every one reads\n\\texttt{[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]}, the three standard\naxioms. Among them, \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 197--201,\nprints the axioms of \\texttt{compiled\\_exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat},\n\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth},\n== sec5:108,120\nThe comparator's settings for the family have \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda:\nfalse}: OpenAI's check of this family ran the Lean kernel only. We ran a\nsecond checker, in the same sandbox, after the build\n(\\repofile{paper3/replay/nanoda.sh}, recorded in the README before it\nran). Two tools are involved. \\texttt{lean4export} (tag\n\\texttt{v4.34.0}, commit \\texttt{076e8e57}, built with the pinned\n\\texttt{v4.34.1} Lean so that it reads the compiled files the build\nproduced) wrote out \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} and everything it\ndepends on from the built environment, as a plain-text file:\n2,355,666,449 bytes, 43,633,742 lines, format version 3.1.0, in\n68~seconds. Then \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib} \\citep{Nanoda} (version 0.4.31,\ncommit \\texttt{8de7e0c4}, 2026-10-29), a checker for Lean~4 proofs\nwritten in Rust that shares no code with the Lean kernel, read that\n== sec5:139,146\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item The replay trusts the Lean kernel of the pinned version\n  (\\texttt{v4.34.1}) and upstream Mathlib at the pinned revision. It\n  moves the proof's trust from ``OpenAI's unpublished build'' to ``a\n  build we ran'', not from the Lean kernel to anything else. The second\n  checker removes the Lean kernel from the list for the theorem's\n  dependencies as 