replay/run_sandbox.sh as paper3/replay/README.md records;\n% the counts below come from `grep -c` on the copied logs, see\n% paper3/replay/logs/run2/counts.txt).\n\\section{Independent replay}\n\\label{sec:replay}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nThe repository holds no saved output of the axiom check for the family,\nno automated build of its Lean, and \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false} in the\ncomparator's settings (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). We therefore built the\nproof ourselves, in isolation, at the Lean version the repository pins,\nand recorded what the kernel reports. This section says exactly what was\nrun, where, and what came out. The scripts are quoted verbatim in\n\\repofile{paper3/replay/README.md}, and the logs are under\n\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/}.\n\n\\subsection{What was run, and where}\n\nA fresh copy of the clone (commit \\texttt{4eaee875}) was built inside a\nsandbox on the project's machine. The sandbox tool is \\texttt{bwrap}\n(bubblewrap). Inside it, the system directories are read-only. The home\ndirectory is a private, empty one (a \\texttt{tmpfs}, a scratch file\nsystem held in memory and gone when the sandbox ends), so nothing of the\nreal home or of this repository is visible. Processes, inter-process\ncommunication and host names are kept apart from the host's (separate\n``namespaces''). The whole sandbox is confined to eight cores\n(\\texttt{taskset -c 0-7}) at the lowest scheduling priority. And the\nnetwork is on, because the repository's build file\n(its \\texttt{lakefile}) clones its 42 dependencies (the packages of its manifest, Section~\\ref{sec:proof}) at build time and the compiled Mathlib comes\nfrom a download cache. Inside the sandbox, the Lean installer\n\\texttt{elan} was installed into the private home and fetched the\npinned Lean version from the repository's own \\texttt{lean-toolchain}\nfile. Then, in the clone's \\texttt{lean/} directory, three commands\nran: \\texttt{lake exe cache get}, which downloads the compiled Mathlib;\n\\texttt{lake build OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}, which builds\nthe solution's 3,091 files and not the collection's default target,\nwhich would build the whole collection (its \\clonefile{README.md}, line 16, counts 721 manuscripts in 373 families); and \\texttt{\\#print axioms} of the\none theorem the comparator names,\n\\path{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.exact_count_and_sat},\nthrough \\texttt{lake env lean}. Only text left the sandbox; nothing\nbuilt inside it was executed outside it.\n\nTwo runs were needed. The first, started at 01:03 on 2026-11-05, failed\nat once at the build step. Its command line carried\n\\texttt{-j 8}, an option this version of the build tool does not have.\nThe whole of its build log is the one line \\texttt{error: unknown short\noption '-j'}, and nothing of the family was compiled. The second, started\nat 01:16 with that option removed and the core cap moved to\n\\texttt{taskset}, is the run reported below. Both scripts, both\nversions, and the launch command are in the README; the first run's\nlogs are kept beside the second's.\n\n\\subsection{What came out}\n\nThe build finished with exit code~0 in 33~minutes of wall clock on\neight cores (01:16:25 to 01:49:31). It compiled \\textbf{3,091} modules\nwith the prefix \\texttt{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT}: exactly the\nset the inspection of Section~\\ref{sec:proof} enumerated, the same\n3,091 names with nothing missing on either side\n(\\repofile{paper3/replay/counts.sh}). The build tool's own job total,\n\\texttt{12014}, counts the cached Mathlib and dependency modules as\nwell, none of which was compiled. The log has no line containing\n\\texttt{error} and none containing \\texttt{sorry}. Its eleven\n\\texttt{warning} lines are the build tool noting that the repository's\ncompatibility patches leave eleven dependencies with ``local changes'',\nand none of the eleven is Mathlib.\n\nEight source files of the closure carry nineteen \\texttt{\\#print\naxioms} commands (seventeen files of the family's directory do, but\nnine of those are never built), so the build log records nineteen\naxiom lines as it goes. Every one reads\n\\texttt{[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]}, the three standard\naxioms. Among them, \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 197--201,\nprints the axioms of \\texttt{compiled\\_exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat},\n\\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat}, \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth},\n\\texttt{sat\\_polynomial\\_space} and \\texttt{original\\_sat\\_main}. The\nseparate \\texttt{\\#print axioms} run after the build gives, for the\ncomparator's theorem,\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n\\path{'OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.exact_count_and_sat'}\\\\\n\\texttt{depends on axioms: [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]}\n\\end{quote}\n(\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/run2/axioms.txt}). The versions are Lean 4.34.1 (the log line reads \\texttt{Lean (version\n4.34.1, commit 5045d005…, Release)}), Lake \\texttt{5.0.0-src+5045d00},\nand Mathlib at revision \\path{d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612}\nfrom the clone's own manifest, with its compiled files from the Mathlib cache (8,908 files).\nThe replay copy is at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}, the same as the pristine\nclone, and the comparator file and the solution's files have the same\nhashes in both (\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/run2/hashes.txt}; the\ncomparator statement's SHA-256, a fingerprint of a file's bytes that two different files do not share in practice, begins \\texttt{4183b205}). The full build\nlog is kept out of the repository by size; its SHA-256\n(\\texttt{d313f594…}) and its last 200 lines are committed.\n\nSo the sentence every later section relies on reads: \\emph{replayed}.\nThe solution builds from source at the pinned Lean version with\nunpatched Mathlib, and the kernel reports the three standard axioms and\nnothing else for the theorem the comparator names. This moves the first\nthree items of the inspection's trust boundary (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}):\nthe build is no longer OpenAI's unpublished one, the axiom output is now\non record, and the Mathlib revision was fetched from upstream and not\npatched.\n\n\\subsection{The second checker}\n\nThe comparator's settings for the family have \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda:\nfalse}: OpenAI's check of this family ran the Lean kernel only. We ran a\nsecond checker, in the same sandbox, after the build\n(\\repofile{paper3/replay/nanoda.sh}, recorded in the README before it\nran). Two tools are involved. \\texttt{lean4export} (tag\n\\texttt{v4.34.0}, commit \\texttt{076e8e57}, built with the pinned\n\\texttt{v4.34.1} Lean so that it reads the compiled files the build\nproduced) wrote out \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} and everything it\ndepends on from the built environment, as a plain-text file:\n2,355,666,449 bytes, 43,633,742 lines, format version 3.1.0, in\n68~seconds. Then \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib} \\citep{Nanoda} (version 0.4.31,\ncommit \\texttt{8de7e0c4}, 2026-10-29), a checker for Lean~4 proofs\nwritten in Rust that shares no code with the Lean kernel, read that\nfile and re-checked every declaration in it, with \\texttt{propext},\n\\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{Quot.sound} as the permitted\naxioms and the \\texttt{Nat} and \\texttt{String} literal extensions on.\nIts output (\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/nanoda/nanoda_check.txt}):\n\\begin{quote}\n\\texttt{Checked 95176 declarations with no errors}\n\\end{quote}\nin 110~seconds on eight cores, exit code~0, after printing back the\ntheorem's statement and the three axioms from its own reading of the\nfile. The export contains exactly three \\texttt{axiom} declarations\n(\\texttt{grep -c '\"axiom\"'} on the file), so nothing else could have\nbeen permitted. The export file (SHA-256 \\texttt{bdbc9afe…},\n\\repofile{paper3/replay/logs/nanoda/export_sha256.txt}) stays in the\nsandbox directory by size; the configuration, the build logs and the\nchecker's output are committed.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item The replay trusts the Lean kernel of the pinned version\n  (\\texttt{v4.34.1}) and upstream Mathlib at the pinned revision. It\n  moves the proof's trust from ``OpenAI's unpublished build'' to ``a\n  build we ran'', not from the Lean kernel to anything else. The second\n  checker removes the Lean kernel from the list for the theorem's\n  dependencies as exported, but adds \\texttt{lean4export} (that it\n  writes out what the environment holds) and \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib} (that\n  it checks what it reads) to it. Two independent checkers agreeing is\n  evidence, not a proof that either is correct.\n\\item It is not the comparator run. The comparator checks that the\n  solution's exported statement matches the challenge's; we did not run\n  that tool chain, and the statement-level match the paper relies on is\n  the textual one of Section~\\ref{sec:proof} (the 35 definitions and the\n  theorem statement, identical after normalising spacing, two of them up\n  to the \\texttt{noncomputable} keyword).\n\\item The counts above are counts of lines in one build's log on one\n  machine with the named versions; a different build tool would print\n  different job totals (the 12,014 includes the cached Mathlib modules),\n  and a different machine a different time.\n\\item Nothing here bears on what the theorem \\emph{says}\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}) or on the manuscript's unverified\n  exponents (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}); a replayed proof of a weak bound\n  is a replayed proof of a weak bound.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\par\\endgroup\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":34,"oldLines":10,"newStart":34,"newLines":10,"lines":[" \\texttt{elan} was installed into the private home and fetched the"," pinned Lean version from the repository's own \\texttt{lean-toolchain}"," file. Then, in the clone's \\texttt{lean/} directory, three commands","-ran: \\texttt{lake exe cache get}, which downloads the compiled Mathlib;","-\\texttt{lake build OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}, which builds","-the solution's 3,091 files and not the collection's default target,","-which would build the whole collection (its \\clonefile{README.md}, line 16, counts 721 manuscripts in 373 families); and \\texttt{\\#print axioms} of the","+ran. The first, \\texttt{lake exe cache get}, downloads the compiled Mathlib.","+The second, \\texttt{lake build OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}, builds","+the solution's 3,091 files. It does not build the collection's default target,","+which would build the whole collection (its \\clonefile{README.md}, line 16, counts 721 manuscripts in 373 families). The third is \\texttt{\\#print axioms} of the"," one theorem the comparator names,"," \\path{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.exact_count_and_sat},"," through \\texttt{lake env lean}. Only text left the sandbox; nothing"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DF1WqgvgWhxuFZFE3z14nv","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
