{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013KaoW8kpLRzYs4L2t3aQKJ","type":"tool_result","content":"== sec6:53,70\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{It is deterministic.} The step rule is a function; there\n  is no random choice and no guess. A deterministic algorithm is also a\n  randomised algorithm, one that happens never to err, and also a\n  nondeterministic algorithm, one that happens never to guess. So each\n  refutation of a hypothesis about deterministic algorithms is a\n  refutation of its randomised and nondeterministic forms by the same\n  machine.\n\\item \\textbf{The bound is subexponential in $n$ alone.} A formula of\n  width $k$ on $n$ variables has at most about $n^k$ distinct clauses\n  ($2{,}604{,}376$ at $n = 125$, $k = 3$), so the input length $L$ is at\n  most a polynomial in $n$, and the whole bound is a polynomial in $n$\n  times $2^{A\\rhon}$. Since $\\rhon/n$ goes to zero\n  \\leanref{rho-little-o}, for every rate $\\delta > 0$ there is a size\n  beyond which the bound is below $2^{\\delta n}$; below that size the\n  bound is a constant depending on $k$ and $\\delta$. In the standard\n  notation, the running time is $O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every\n  $\\delta > 0$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-hypotheses}). This is a fact\n== sec6:178,186\n\\paragraph{NSETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 181--183): ``The Nondeterministic Strong\nExponential Time Hypothesis (NSETH) asserts that, for every\n$\\varepsilon > 0$, some fixed $k$ admits no nondeterministic\n$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}\\allowbreak(n+m)^{O(1)}$-time algorithm for deciding whether a\n$k$-CNF is unsatisfiable, or equivalently whether a $k$-DNF is a\ntautology [CGI$^+$16],'' citing \\citet{Carmosino2016}. (A $k$-DNF is the mirror image of a $k$-CNF, an ``or'' of small ``and''s, and a tautology is a formula true under every assignment.) In words: even an algorithm allowed to guess should not be able to certify\nunsatisfiability with a saving over $2^n$. Its denial: there is a saving\nthat every fixed $k$ allows, for a guessing algorithm. The verified\n== sec6:249,262\nThe manuscript's \\S1.1.3 opens with a list of lower bounds that were\nproved from ETH or from SETH (p.~5, lines 213--225), for problems with\nexponential algorithms and for problems with polynomial ones, and then\nsays what its result does to them (lines 226--227):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``Our refutation of ETH and SETH removes one basis for the hardness\nclaims above. Some of the same lower bounds follow from weaker\nhypotheses that may still hold.''\n\\end{quote}\nWe say what that means in general terms, for a theorem of either of the\ntwo standard shapes,\n\\begin{align*}\n  \\text{(E)}&\\quad \\text{ETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } 2^{o(n)} \\text{ algorithm for } X,\\\\\n  \\text{(S)}&\\quad \\text{SETH} \\Rightarrow \\text{no } (c - \\varepsilon)^{k} \\text{ algorithm for } Y \\text{ for any } \\varepsilon > 0,\n== sec6:318,334\n\\paragraph{The weaker hypotheses the manuscript names are untouched.}\nThe manuscript gives two (p.~5, lines 229--236). The first is the\nOrthogonal Vectors Conjecture, which it states and then relates to SETH:\n``The reduction from SAT to Orthogonal Vectors shows that SETH implies\nOVC [Wil05, VW19]'' (lines 231--232). That implication is a theorem\nwith a false premise, like (E) and (S): it says nothing about OVC either\nway, and nothing in the verified statement is about orthogonal vectors.\nThe second is the form of SETH for formulas of unrestricted width, which\nwe quote with the manuscript's list of the problems it covers elided\n(lines 233--236):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``The lower bounds for [\\ldots] also follow from the hypothesis that\nunrestricted CNF-SAT admits no $2^{(1-\\delta)n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n,m)$-time\nalgorithm for any constant $\\delta > 0$ [Bri14, RV13, LMS18]. Theorem\n1.1 gives a running-time guarantee for each fixed clause width $k$,\nwhile this hypothesis allows the width to grow with $n$.''\n\\end{quote}\n== sec7:14,20\nThe title compares two guarantees. One is the verified bound,\n$C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, with $C$, $A$ and $d$ never computed (comparator\nfile lines 136--139 and 210--211, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The\nother is the trivial bound for trying every assignment, $2^n$ times a\npolynomial. Neither is what a practitioner runs. What runs on a real\nmachine, when the question is whether a formula can be satisfied, is a\n\\emph{SAT solver}; the standard kind is called CDCL, for\n== sec7:34,40\nThe verified statement says a machine \\emph{exists} (comparator line 195\nopens with the existence quantifier, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The\nsolution's 3,977 files contain no command to run anything: no\n\\texttt{\\#eval}, no \\texttt{def main} and no input-output (\\texttt{IO})\naction. The\nscript of this section checks that, reading every file of the family\nwith \\texttt{open()} and counting those three patterns, each as a whole\n== sec7:88,96\n\\paragraph{Protocol.} Each call runs in its own interpreter under\n\\texttt{nice -n 19}, at most eight at a time on the thirty-two cores of\nan AMD Ryzen 9 9950X (Linux 7.0.0, Python 3.12.13; the machine was\nshared with other work, load average at the start in\n\\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}), with a wall-clock budget of\n60~s per call. The time recorded is the wall clock (\\texttt{perf\\_counter}) around the\nconstruction of the solver object and the one call, inside the child\nprocess, with the interpreter's start-up and the import outside it. A\ncall over budget is killed and recorded as \\emph{censored}, that is, as\n== sec7:104,110\nTable~\\ref{tab:practitioner} gives, per size, the number of satisfiable\ninstances, CaDiCaL's median and maximum over the twenty seeds, the\nnumber of instances each counter finished with its median and maximum\n(a median over all twenty with censored runs counted as above the\nbudget, so a median is a number whenever more than ten finished), and\nthe two guarantees in the common scale of the next subsection.\nFigure~\\ref{fig:practitioner} draws the same.\n== sec7:180,186\nThe guarantees are drawn as shapes. Both are taken with constant $1$\n(the polynomial factors, $C$, $A$ and $d$ are all set aside), and both\nare multiplied by the same factor, chosen so that $2^n$ passes through\nCaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$. Because the factor is one number for both\ncurves, their ratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves,\n$2^{\\rhon - n}$: below $1$ for $n < 50$, equal to $1$ at $n = 50$ up to\nrounding, and above $1$ from there (it is $2.8 \\times 10^{3}$ at\n== sec7:194,200\nIn that scale the brute-force guarantee reaches one second near\n$n = 33$ and the verified guarantee near $n = 35$. At $n = 60$, the\nmeasured CaDiCaL median is $3 \\times 10^{-4}$~s, against $1.4 \\times\n10^{8}$~s for $2^n$ and $5.4 \\times 10^{8}$~s for $2^{\\rhon}$: the\nmeasurement is below the two guarantees by factors of $10^{11.6}$ and\n$10^{12.2}$. At $n = 500$ the decision median is above the budget (8 of\n20 finished within 60~s), against $10^{140.6}$~s and $10^{192.2}$~s.\n== sec7:210,216\nOne remark on what the verified machine's \\emph{running time} would\nhave to be, were it ever run. Nothing in this section bears on it: the\nconstants $C, A, d$ are unknown (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), the\nmachine is a mathematical term, and a statement that something exists\nis not a program. The only values on record are the proof's own:\n$A \\ge 257$ in its construction (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), and\n$A \\ge 258$ with $C \\ge 28{,}701$ in the theorem it exhibits","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17474a62-7527-453d-99c4-703178c27619","uuid":"b7816a1a-e53b-44be-b045-7af26eb1e4e6","timestamp":"2026-11-05T09:14:18.964Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"== sec6:53,70\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{It is deterministic.} The step rule is a function; there\n  is no random choice and no guess. A deterministic algorithm i