{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NouNa3UcHd6hPLDXUv7U6U","type":"tool_result","content":"== sec6_consequences.tex:186,200\nthat every fixed $k$ allows, for a guessing algorithm. The verified\nmachine's answer bit is \\texttt{false} exactly when the count is zero\n(lines 205--207), so it decides unsatisfiability deterministically within\nthe SETH bound of the previous paragraph for every $\\varepsilon$ and\nevery $k$; and a deterministic machine is a guessing machine that never\nguesses, so every convention for nondeterministic time is met. The\nmanuscript's ``decides both satisfiability and unsatisfiability'' (line\n188) is this.\n\n\\paragraph{\\#ETH.}\nAs stated (p.~4, lines 204--205): ``The counting Exponential Time\nHypothesis (\\#ETH) asserted that exactly counting the satisfying\nassignments of a 3-CNF requires $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ deterministic time\n[DHM$^+$14, Section 1],'' citing \\citet{Dell2014}. Its denial: for every\nrate $\\delta > 0$ there is a deterministic algorithm within\n== sec7_practitioner.tex:38,46\naction. The\nscript of this section checks that, reading every file of the family\nwith \\texttt{open()} and counting those three patterns, each as a whole\nword at the start of a line or before a type (so \\texttt{def mainProgram}\nis not \\texttt{def main}): 0, 0, 0. The\nlibrary proves that a machine with the stated bound exists and exhibits\none as a mathematical term, inside a development with 1,048\n\\texttt{noncomputable} declarations and no way to execute anything\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); nothing in the clone runs it on an input, and\n== sec7_practitioner.tex:188,192\nthe $n = 20$ point on its own would instead make the two touch at\n$n = 20$ and put the verified one above from there on; the common\nfactor is the honest choice because it keeps the crossing where the\nstatement puts it (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-scaling};\n\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a).\n== sec7_practitioner.tex:205,210\ntitle is the order of the three objects: at every size in the table the\nmeasurement is far below $2^n$, and $2^{\\rhon}$ is above $2^n$ from\n$n = 50$ on, so the verified guarantee is the largest of the three at\nevery practical size and the measurement the smallest.\n\nOne remark on what the verified machine's \\emph{running time} would\n== sec8_lean.tex:42,47\nlines. Lines 1--190 define the objects the theorem speaks about: the\nformulas, their encoding as a string of bits, the multitape machine, its\nstep and its starting configuration, the exponent $\\runtimeScale$ and\nthe bound built from it, the count word, and the bookkeeping that\nrecords which tape cells a run has visited. Lines 191--219 state the\ntheorem \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} and end it with \\texttt{sorry},\n== sec8_lean.tex:64,68\nhas no \\texttt{sorry} and no axiom declaration outside its comments (the\nsame script counts them: zero and zero in each of our two files; the two\nwords do occur in comments, \\texttt{PrimitiveMachine.lean} line 10 and\n\\texttt{Frame.lean} lines 13 and 83, saying that nothing is one). Everything in the\ncopy is a definition. A definition can be badly chosen, but it cannot be\n== sec8_lean.tex:88,94\nassignments, answers whether there is one, runs within the bound built\nfrom $\\runtimeScale$, and visits only polynomially many cells.\n\nThe same script checks that the body is the comparator's. It takes the\n23 lines after \\texttt{def SubexpCount (k : }$\\mathbb{N}$\\texttt{) : Prop :=}\nin our file (lines 85--107). It takes the 23 lines after the theorem's\nleading parts in the clone (lines 195--217, with the closing \\texttt{:= by}\n== sec8_lean.tex:166,170\nthree standard axioms; \\texttt{Trace} on no axiom at all (it is defined by construction rules and carries no proof); \\texttt{countWord} on two of the three (a proof may use fewer than the three, and fewer is never a concern); \\texttt{sorryAx} on none. The library it runs against is built with\nLean~4.34.0-rc