in Appendix B.\n\\section{For the practitioner}\n\\label{sec:practitioner}\n\nThe title compares two guarantees. One is the verified bound,\n$C(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$, with $C$, $A$ and $d$ never computed (comparator\nfile lines 136--139 and 210--211, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The\nother is the trivial bound for trying every assignment, $2^n$ times a\npolynomial. Neither is what a practitioner runs. What runs on a real\nmachine, when the question is whether a formula can be satisfied, is a\n\\emph{SAT solver}; the standard kind is called CDCL, for\nconflict-driven clause learning, a search that learns from each dead\nend it reaches. When the question is how many satisfying assignments\nthere are, what runs is a \\emph{model counter}, exact or approximate.\nNo implementation of the verified machine exists to run beside them.\nThis section measures the tools a practitioner has, on random formulas,\nand draws the two guarantees next to the measurements as shapes, so\nthat the reader can see where each sits. The point is small and the section says it once. The verified guarantee is not the binding one on any machine, because it is above $2^n$ for every $n \\ge 50$\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The solvers, meanwhile, are many orders of\nmagnitude below $2^n$ at every size they were run at.\n\n\\subsection{Why the comparison is between guarantees and measurements}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-why}\n\nThe verified statement says a machine \\emph{exists} (comparator line 195\nopens with the existence quantifier, Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The\nsolution's 3,977 files contain no command to run anything: no\n\\texttt{\\#eval}, no \\texttt{def main} and no input-output (\\texttt{IO})\naction. The\nscript of this section checks that, reading every file of the family\nwith \\texttt{open()} and counting those three patterns, each as a whole\nword at the start of a line or before a type (so \\texttt{def mainProgram}\nis not \\texttt{def main}): 0, 0, 0. The\nlibrary proves that a machine with the stated bound exists, and exhibits\none as a mathematical term. That term sits inside a development with 1,048\n\\texttt{noncomputable} declarations and no way to execute anything\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). Nothing in the clone runs it on an input, and\nwe found no implementation elsewhere. So the verified algorithm's actual\nrunning time on any formula is unknown, in both directions: nothing says\nit is slow, and nothing says it is fast. What is known is its\n\\emph{guarantee}, and a guarantee is compared with other guarantees. The\nmeasurements below are of other programs entirely. They are in the\nsection because the question a practitioner asks is not ``which\nguarantee is smaller'' but ``what would I run'', and the answer to that\nhas not changed.\n\n\\subsection{What was run}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-setup}\n\n\\paragraph{Instances.} Random 3-CNF on $n$ variables with $m = \\lfloor\n4.26\\,n \\rceil$ clauses ($4.26\\,n$ rounded to the nearest whole number), each clause three distinct variables with\nrandom signs, generated by the script from $(n, \\mathrm{seed})$; twenty\nseeds per size; $n = 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 80, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300,\n400, 500$. The ratio $4.26$ clauses per variable is where random 3-SAT\nswitches from mostly satisfiable to mostly unsatisfiable\n\\citep{Selman1996Hard}; random formulas are hardest to decide there\n(a method from statistical physics puts the threshold at $4.2667$, \\citealp{Mertens2006Threshold}). The sizes are chosen so that the\nsmallest ones are where the title's crossing is ($n = 50$) and the\nlargest are where a CDCL solver on random formulas begins to take\nseconds. These are random instances only; nothing is claimed about\nformulas with structure.\n\n\\paragraph{Tools.} CaDiCaL 1.9.5 \\citep{Biere2024CaDiCaL}, a CDCL solver,\ngives the yes-or-no answer, through python-sat 1.9.dev15\n\\citep{Ignatiev2018PySAT} in one \\texttt{solve()} call. Ganak 2.8.0\n\\citep{Sharma2019Ganak}, an exact model counter, gives the exact count,\nthrough the pyganak binding (which runs a simplification pass called\nArjun and then the counter). ApproxMC 4.4.0 \\citep{Chakraborty2013ApproxMC,\nSoos2019BIRD}, an approximate counter, runs at its defaults $\\varepsilon =\n0.8$, $\\delta = 0.2$ (an answer within a factor $1.8$ of the truth with\nprobability $0.8$), with the count taken over all variables (its\n``projection set''). And at $n \\le 30$ only, the exact count is also\nfound by listing every solution with CaDiCaL, one at a time. That is not\na counter at any larger size, since its time grows with the count and the\ncount grows exponentially in $n$; it is here as the independent check of\nthe two counters where it can reach. Ganak and ApproxMC were installed from PyPI for this section; no\n\\texttt{sharpSAT}, \\texttt{d4} or other counter binary is on the host.\n\n\\paragraph{Protocol.} Each call runs in its own interpreter under\n\\texttt{nice -n 19}, at most eight at a time, with a wall-clock budget of\n60~s per call. The machine is an AMD Ryzen 9 9950X with thirty-two cores\n(Linux 7.0.0, Python 3.12.13). It was\nshared with other work; the load average at the start is in\n\\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}. The time recorded is the wall clock (\\texttt{perf\\_counter}) around the\nconstruction of the solver object and the one call, inside the child\nprocess, with the interpreter's start-up and the import outside it. A\ncall over budget is killed and recorded as \\emph{censored}, that is, as\n``more than 60~s''. A size at which a tool finishes fewer than ten of\nits twenty instances ends that tool's climb to larger sizes. The whole\nrun took about twenty minutes (1,172~s).\n\n\\subsection{What came out}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-results}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:practitioner} gives, per size, four things. First, the number of satisfiable\ninstances. Second, CaDiCaL's median and maximum over the twenty seeds. Third, the\nnumber of instances each counter finished, with its median and maximum;\na median is taken over all twenty, with censored runs counted as above the\nbudget, so it is a number whenever more than ten finished. Fourth,\nthe two guarantees in the common scale of the next subsection.\nFigure~\\ref{fig:practitioner} draws the same.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Random 3-CNF at $m = \\lfloor 4.26\\,n \\rceil$, twenty seeds per\nsize, one machine, 60~s per call. Columns: $n$ variables; $m$ clauses;\n``sat'', how many of the twenty instances CaDiCaL found satisfiable (at\n$n = 400$ and $500$ only 10 and 8 finished, every one of them\nsatisfiable); for each tool, ``fin'', the number of instances it finished\nwithin budget, and ``med'' and ``max'', its median and maximum seconds\nover all twenty seeds with a censored run counted as $> 60$. The last\ntwo columns are the two guarantees as shapes: $2^n$ (trying every\nassignment) and $2^{\\rhon}$ (the verified bound), both with constant\n$1$ and both multiplied by one common factor $t_{20}/2^{20}$ that puts\n$2^n$ through CaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$ ($t_{20} = 1.28 \\times\n10^{-4}$~s). They are shapes, not running times. Regenerate:\n\\texttt{python3 -I paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n\\label{tab:practitioner}\n\\footnotesize\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n\\input{tables/practitioner}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{practitioner.pdf}\n\\caption{Table~\\ref{tab:practitioner}'s data on a logarithmic time axis, where each grid line\nis ten times the one below. Solid lines with markers are medians, dotted\nlines maxima, over the twenty seeds; a tool whose median is censored\nstops at the budget line. The two guarantees are drawn as shapes with\nthe common factor of Table~\\ref{tab:practitioner}, so that $2^n$ passes\nthrough the $n = 20$ measurement and $2^{\\rhon}$ crosses it at $n = 50$,\nwhere the verified exponent overtakes $n$\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). The left panel keeps the measured\nseconds in view and lets the guarantees leave the frame; the right\npanel is the same picture on the guarantees' own scale. From\n\\texttt{paper3/scripts/practitioner.py}.}\n\\label{fig:practitioner}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\paragraph{The checks.} On every instance where both finished, Ganak's\ncount equals the count by listing (40 instances at $n \\le 30$) and\nCaDiCaL's answer equals ``count $> 0$''. ApproxMC's estimate is within\nits factor $1 + \\varepsilon = 1.8$ of Ganak's on 178 of the 206\ninstances where both finished (it promises that with probability\n$1 - \\delta = 0.8$ per instance). The script exits non-zero if any of\nthese fails.\n\n\\paragraph{What the solvers did.} CaDiCaL's median is under a\nmillisecond up to $n = 80$, 2.5~ms at 100, 58~ms at 200, 1.5~s at 250\nand 3.7~s at 300 (maximum 20.5~s). At 400 it finishes 10 of 20 within\nthe budget and at 500 8 of 20, every finished one satisfiable. That is\nthe known shape of CDCL on random formulas at the threshold: the\nunsatisfiable half is the expensive half. Ganak finishes all 20 up to\n$n = 250$ (median 17.6~s there, maximum 45.7~s), 10 of 20 at 300 and\nnone at 400; its median is flat at 8.3~s and 6.2~s at $n = 100$ and\n150 and lower, 2.4~s, at 200, while its maximum rises throughout. The\nflat stretch looks like a fixed time budget inside the binding's\npreprocessing rather than counting difficulty; we report it as measured\nand tuned nothing (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06d). Where the count is\npositive it is small for the size: at $n = 200$ the fifteen satisfiable\ninstances have between 372 and $2.5 \\times 10^{12}$ solutions out of\n$2^{200}$ assignments. ApproxMC finishes all 20 up to $n = 200$ (median\n0.51~s there), 18 at 250, 11 at 300 and none at 400. The sizes at which\nthe three tools leave the budget, 300 for the counters and 400 for the\ndecision, are where this measurement ends; nothing is claimed beyond\nthem.\n\n\\subsection{The two guarantees beside them}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-guarantees}\n\nThe guarantees are drawn as shapes. Both are taken with constant $1$\n(the polynomial factors, $C$, $A$ and $d$ are all set aside), and both\nare multiplied by the same factor, chosen so that $2^n$ passes through\nCaDiCaL's median at $n = 20$. Because the factor is one number for both\ncurves, their ratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves,\n$2^{\\rhon - n}$: below $1$ for $n < 50$, equal to $1$ at $n = 50$ up to\nrounding, and above $1$ from there (it is $2.8 \\times 10^{3}$ at\n$n = 100$ and $4.0 \\times 10^{51}$ at $n = 500$). Scaling each curve to\nthe $n = 20$ point on its own would instead make the two touch at\n$n = 20$ and put the verified one above from there on. The common\nfactor is the honest choice, because it keeps the crossing where the\nstatement puts it (Appendix~\\ref{app:tech-scaling};\n\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a).\n\nIn that scale the brute-force guarantee reaches one second near\n$n = 33$ and the verified guarantee near $n = 35$. At $n = 60$, the\nmeasured CaDiCaL median is $3 \\times 10^{-4}$~s, against $1.4 \\times\n10^{8}$~s for $2^n$ and $5.4 \\times 10^{8}$~s for $2^{\\rhon}$: the\nmeasurement is below the two guarantees by factors of $10^{11.6}$ and\n$10^{12.2}$. At $n = 500$ the decision median is above the budget (8 of\n20 finished within 60~s), against $10^{140.6}$~s and $10^{192.2}$~s.\nNothing in those enormous factors is about the verified machine: the\ngap is between what a CDCL solver does on random formulas and what any\nexponential guarantee says, and it would be the same gap for the\nbrute-force guarantee alone. The one fact this section adds to the\ntitle is the order of the three objects. At every size in the table the\nmeasurement is far below $2^n$, and $2^{\\rhon}$ is above $2^n$ from\n$n = 50$ on. So the verified guarantee is the largest of the three at\nevery practical size, and the measurement the smallest.\n\nOne remark on what the verified machine's \\emph{running time} would\nhave to be, were it ever run. Nothing in this section bears on it: the\nconstants $C, A, d$ are unknown (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), the\nmachine is a mathematical term, and a statement that something exists\nis not a program. The only values on record are the proof's own:\n$A \\ge 257$ in its construction (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), and\n$A \\ge 258$ with $C \\ge 28{,}701$ in the theorem it exhibits\n(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); a guarantee of $2^{257\\,\\rhon}$ steps is a guarantee\nand not a measurement, and a different proof of the same statement\ncould carry any positive constants.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:practitioner-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Timings on one machine, one day, named versions.} The\n  seconds are from one AMD Ryzen 9 9950X shared with other work, under\n  \\texttt{nice -n 19} with eight calls in flight, through Python\n  bindings; they are reproducible in shape and not in value, and the\n  versions are those named in Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner-setup} and\n  in \\repofile{tables/practitioner_meta.json}.\n\\item \\textbf{Random instances only.} Random 3-CNF at the threshold\n  ratio is one distribution; formulas with structure can be harder for\n  CDCL at far smaller $n$ and easier for counters. Nothing is claimed\n  about any other distribution, and nothing about worst-case behaviour.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the verified machine's running time.} It is\n  unknown, in both directions; no implementation exists to run; the\n  guarantees drawn are at constant $1$ and the constants are never\n  computed.\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about $k > 3$.} The statement is for every fixed\n  $k$; the measurement is at $k = 3$.\n\\item \\textbf{The counters are reported, not tuned.} Ganak ran through\n  its binding at defaults, preprocessing included; ApproxMC at its\n  default tolerance; a different configuration gives different seconds.\n  The check against listing is at $n \\le 30$ only.\n\\item \\textbf{No statement about which tool a practitioner should use.}\n  The section shows that the verified guarantee is not the binding one;\n  it does not rank solvers.\n\\end{itemize}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":214,"oldLines":8,"newStart":214,"newLines":8,"lines":[" is not a program. The only values on record are the proof's own:"," $A \\ge 257$ in its construction (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}), and"," $A \\ge 258$ with $C \\ge 28{,}701$ in the theorem it exhibits","-(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}); a guarantee of $2^{257\\,\\rhon}$ steps is a guarantee","-and not a measurement, and a different proof of the same statement","+(Section~\\ref{sec:proof}). A guarantee of $2^{257\\,\\rhon}$ steps is a guarantee","+and not a measurement. A different proof of the same statement"," could carry any positive constants."," "," \\subsection{What this does not claim}"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018pWwEiXHvJX4vMwUL8JjX4","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
