a published human audit of an earlier\nbatch of OpenAI's results. Fourth, the checking tool, called the comparator. Fifth,\nthe second proof checker, called nanoda, that we ran in\nSection~\\ref{sec:replay}. The section ends by saying where we looked for\nan earlier statement of the title's observation and what we found.\nTable~\\ref{tab:related-sources} lists every source with where it is on\ndisk, when it was fetched, and its hash or commit. We quote only what is\non disk; nothing in this section is quoted from memory or from the press.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{The sources of this section. ``Clone'' is the read-only copy of\nthe OpenAI collection at commit \\texttt{4eaee875}; ``fetched'' files were\ndownloaded on 2026-11-05 and are kept under \\texttt{paper3/related/fetched/}\nwith their SHA-256 hashes. Written by\n\\texttt{paper3/scripts/related\\_work.py}.}\n\\label{tab:related-sources}\n\\input{tables/related_sources}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{What the collection says about itself}\n\\label{sec:related-collection}\n\nThe collection keeps a dated log, \\clonefile{history.md} (56 lines). Under\nthe heading ``October 26, 2026'' (line 11), the entry for this family is\none bullet, which we quote in full (line 18):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``\\emph{The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False.} The paper gives a\ndeterministic algorithm that exactly counts satisfying assignments of\nfixed-width CNF formulas in subexponential time and polynomial space. The\ncase of 3-SAT refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis. An independent\nworking group of computer scientists led by Russell Impagliazzo,\nRamamohan Paturi, and Ryan Williams will examine and explain the proof.\nAt the group’s request, we are making the materials public immediately so\nothers can examine them as well.''\n\\end{quote}\nThree things in this sentence matter for us. First, the group it names is\nled by two of the three authors of the papers that stated the hypotheses\n(Section~\\ref{sec:related-hypotheses}). Second, the verb is ``will'': the\nlog announces an examination to come. Nothing from that group is in the\nclone, and we fetched nothing from it, so this paper has no finding of\ntheirs to cite, agree with or disagree with. Whatever that group\npublishes about the proof will carry more weight on the proof than\nSection~\\ref{sec:proof} does, which only inspected and replayed. Third,\nthe log says ``the paper gives \\ldots{} subexponential time'' with no\nexponent; the exponent is in the scope note.\n\nThat scope note is \\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, seventeen lines, quoted\nin full in Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}. It is the collection's own statement\nof the gap between what Lean proves (the exponent with the squared\n$\\log\\log$ factor) and what the manuscript claims (two sharper\nexponents). Its table (line 17) links the result to the one comparator\nstatement, \\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean}.\nSo the collection itself records that the verified exponent is the weaker\none. What it does not record is what the weaker exponent means at any\nsize. The verified exponent $\\rhon$ is larger than $n$ for every\n$n$ between $50$ and $e^{5504}$. So the verified bound with constant\none sits above $2^n$ at every size anyone will ever run\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). That is the title's observation, and the\nnote does not make it.\n\nThe third document is \\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/README.md},\nten lines. It says how the collection's checks are run. First: ``Install\n\\texttt{comparator}, \\texttt{landrun}, and \\texttt{lean4export}, and make\nthem available on \\texttt{PATH}.'' (line 3). Then, from the \\texttt{lean/}\ndirectory, \\texttt{lake update}, \\texttt{lake exe cache get}, and\n\\texttt{lake env comparator} on a family's JSON (lines 7--9; the example\nis another family's). The JSON for this family has six fields\n(Section~\\ref{sec:related-comparator} reads them).\n\n\\subsection{The two papers that stated the hypotheses}\n\\label{sec:related-hypotheses}\n\nETH, the Exponential Time Hypothesis, says that 3-SAT (deciding whether a\nformula whose clauses have three literals each can be satisfied) has no\nalgorithm whose running time grows slower than every exponential in the\nnumber of variables. SETH, the Strong form, says that as the clause width\ngrows, the best possible base of the exponential tends to $2$, the base of\ntrying every assignment. Both are from \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}; the\nstandard setting of SETH, and the lemma that lets one move between the\nnumber of variables and the number of clauses, are from\n\\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}. The manuscript cites both as\n\\texttt{[IP01]} and \\texttt{[IPZ01]} (extract page 66, lines 3733--3736)\nand states each hypothesis in its own words in its \\S1.1.1, which\nSection~\\ref{sec:consequences} quotes with page and line. We do not hold\neither paper: \\repofile{literature/} has no file for them, and\n\\repofile{literature/MISSING.md} now records both with their DOIs. Every\nstatement of a hypothesis in this paper is therefore the manuscript's\nstatement of it, not the original's, and we quote nothing from the two\noriginals. The manuscript's own account of what motivated SETH is one\nsentence we can locate (page 4, lines 170--171): the improvements of the\nknown algorithms ``over exhaustive search diminish as $k$ grows [IP01,\nOpen Problems, item 2]''. Exhaustive search is the ``trying all $2^n$\nassignments'' of the title.\n\n\\subsection{A human audit of an earlier batch of results}\n\\label{sec:related-audit}\n\n\\citet{SienickiSienicki2026} is a published human audit of OpenAI's\nAI-generated proofs. We fetched its arXiv abstract page and API record\n(v1 of 3 August 2026, v2 of 25 August, v3 of 9 September 2026; 13 pages;\nprimary class cs.AI). It is not about this family. Its abstract says\nwhat it covers: ``We assess 18 chapter-specific reviews of the ten\nmathematical results announced by OpenAI on 1 August 2026, alongside\nreview standards, Lean formalizations, subsequent research, and\nmathematical references.'' The family this paper reads is dated 17\nOctober 2026, revised 25 October, and entered the collection on 26\nOctober (Section~\\ref{sec:related-collection}); it is not one of the ten,\nand the abstract mentions neither it nor ETH (the script checks). So we\ncite the audit for its method of review and for nothing about the proof.\n\nFour sentences of the abstract bear on how this paper was written. On\nrestraint: ``The article audits this review record without claiming a\ncomplete reconstruction of all ten proofs.'' On the finding: ``No\nconfirmed substantive mathematical error in a principal result remains in\nthe examined assessments, although review depth varies and some\ndependencies remain partly checked.'' On a hazard of reading proofs\nthrough text extraction: ``In Chapter 6, an apparent polarity error was\nwithdrawn after an overbar lost during PDF extraction was recovered from\nthe typeset source.'' And on what confidence should rest on: ``We argue\nthat confidence should combine formal checking, human reconstruction,\nindependent mathematical use, and a public record supporting correction\nof both proofs and reviews.''\n\nThe third of these is a warning we took. This paper reads the manuscript\nthrough a \\texttt{pdftotext} extract, the same kind of text in which that\noverbar was lost. That is why every quotation from the manuscript carries\nthe page of the PDF beside the line of the extract. It is why the Lean is\nread from its source files and never from a rendering. And it is why no\nclaim in this paper rests on a formula read from the extract alone: the\nformulas\nthat matter are the Lean's, re-typed in Section~\\ref{sec:statement} from\nthe \\texttt{.lean} file and re-checked by the kernel in\nSection~\\ref{sec:lean}. Against the fourth sentence, this paper is one of\nthe four things it lists, formal checking, replayed by us and by a\nsecond kernel, together with a reading of what the checked statement\nsays. It is not a human reconstruction of the proof: Section~\\ref{sec:proof}\ninspected the proof's files and did not re-derive the argument. We\nfetched the abstract only, and quote nothing from the paper's body.\n\n\\subsection{The comparator}\n\\label{sec:related-comparator}\n\nThe comparator is the program the collection used to check that the\nsolution file proves the challenge statement\n\\citep{LeanComparator}. We fetched its README from the\n\\texttt{master} branch of \\texttt{leanprover/comparator} on 2026-11-05\n(latest commit \\texttt{865edc14} of 2026-10-14, ``doc: migration\nnotice''; the repository is archived and under the Apache-2.0 licence).\nIts first lines say the tool has moved: ``This project has been merged\ninto core as of Lean release v4.35.0 in the form of \\texttt{lake\ncomparator} and the accompanying \\texttt{lake check}'' (line 3). The\ncollection pins Lean v4.34.1 (Section~\\ref{sec:proof}), one release\nearlier, so the stand-alone tool is the one its README names. The README\nopens: ``Comparator is a trustworthy judge for Lean proofs.'' (line 8).\n\nWhat it checks is stated as three guarantees for the theorems a JSON\nconfiguration names (lines 52--55):\n\\begin{quote}\\small\n``All theorems in \\texttt{Solution} that are listed in\n\\texttt{theorem\\_names} are guaranteed to:\\\\\n1. Prove the same statement as provided in \\texttt{Challenge}\\\\\n2. Use no more axioms than listed in \\texttt{permitted\\_axioms}\\\\\n3. Be accepted by the Lean kernel''\n\\end{quote}\nIn plain words: the solution proves exactly the sentence\nthe challenge posed, uses only the agreed axioms (the three standard ones\nin the example configuration, line 27), and passes the kernel, the small\ncore of Lean that checks proofs. How it checks them is in its\n``Internals'' (lines 184--200), six steps: build the challenge inside a\nsandbox; export its declarations with \\texttt{lean4export}; do the same\nfor the solution; ``Verify that all declarations used in the statement of\nall relevant theorems in \\texttt{Challenge} are the same as in the\n\\texttt{Solution} environment'' (lines 191--192); ``Verify that the body\nof all relevant theorems in the \\texttt{Solution} environment only uses\naxioms listed in \\texttt{permitted\\_axioms}'' (lines 195--196); and\n``Replay the \\texttt{Solution} environment into the Lean kernel'' (line\n197). The first guarantee is therefore a match of the statement's\ndeclarations as exported, not a textual comparison of the two files.\nThat is the check Section~\\ref{sec:proof} says we did not reproduce: our\nstatement match is textual (Table~\\ref{tab:proof-trust}, row (iv)).\n\nThe guarantees hold under six stated assumptions (lines 34--45). The one\nthat concerns the kernel is the fifth: ``The Lean kernel is correct (with\n\\texttt{external\\_kernels} this can be reduced to `At least one of the\nLean kernel or the \\texttt{external\\_kernels} is correct')'' (lines\n43--44). The sandbox is \\texttt{landrun} (line 10), a tool that confines\nthe build so that a malicious solution file cannot tamper with the\nchecker. The README notes that its trusted base ``naturally includes the\noperating system and hardware it is running on, plus its sandboxing\nmechanism'' (line 58). External kernels are optional: ``Comparator can\nadditionally check solutions with external kernels'' (line 70), and ``For\nbackwards compatibility reasons users may instead set\n\\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: true} to obtain a config that calls\n\\texttt{nanoda\\_bin}'' (lines 86--87). The tool ``was originally\ndeveloped by Lean FRO, with feedback from the AIMO team, in support of\nthe AIMO series of competitions'' (lines 207--208).\n\nFor this family the configuration has six fields\n(\\clonefile{lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.json}): the\nchallenge module; the solution module,\n\\path{OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main}; one theorem name,\n\\path{SqrtLogSpace.exact_count_and_sat}; an empty\n\\texttt{definition\\_names}; the three standard axioms as\n\\texttt{permitted\\_axioms}; and \\texttt{enable\\_nanoda: false}. There is\nno \\texttt{external\\_kernels} field. So the check the collection\nconfigured for this family is the Lean kernel alone, under the fifth\nassumption in its unweakened form. Section~\\ref{sec:replay} is the\nweakened form, supplied by us: the same theorem's dependencies through a\nsecond kernel. Our \\texttt{bwrap} sandbox there played the role\n\\texttt{landrun} plays here, for the same reason, and we did not run the\ncomparator itself.\n\n\\subsection{nanoda}\n\\label{sec:related-nanoda}\n\nnanoda is the second checker \\citep{Nanoda}. Its README was fetched from\nthe \\texttt{master} branch of \\texttt{ammkrn/nanoda\\_lib} on 2026-11-05.\nIt says what it is in one sentence (line 3): ``This is an external type\nchecker for the Lean 4 programming language and theorem prover.'' The\nrepository's description line is ``Library implementing type\ninference/checking functionality based on the Lean theorem prover''; the\nlicence is Apache-2.0. It reads the file \\texttt{lean4export} writes:\n``Export files can be created with lean4export'' (line 13), and ``Given\nthat lean4export already uses json as its export format, adding a json\nparser as a dependency doesn't increase the overall complexity of the\nproject'' (line 23). Its axiom rule is the comparator's: ``The average\nLean user will want to permit the three semi-official axioms, which are\n\\texttt{Quot.sound}, \\texttt{Classical.choice}, and \\texttt{propext}, and\nmost export files will also rely on \\texttt{Lean.trustCompiler}'' (line\n33). The export we checked contains the first three and no fourth\n(Section~\\ref{sec:replay}). Two kernel extensions for arithmetic on\nliterals are off by default and ``enable or disable the Nat and String\nkernel extensions'' (line 39); the replay turned both on.\n\nThe latest commit of the repository at the time of the fetch is\n\\texttt{8de7e0c4} of 2026-10-29, and that is the commit the replay built\nand ran (version 0.4.31; the script checks the replay's log against the\nfetched record). The checker is written in Rust and shares no code with\nthe Lean kernel, which is what makes it a second opinion. Its author's\nbook on external type checkers, linked from line 3 of the README, was not\nfetched.\n\n\\subsection{Where we looked for an earlier statement of the title's observation}\n\\label{sec:related-search}\n\nThe title says the verified guarantee is weaker than trying all $2^n$\nassignments at every practical size. We looked for an earlier statement\nof that observation in the texts this paper rests on, and in no others:\nthe collection's \\clonefile{history.md}, its scope note\n\\clonefile{lean/docs/378.md}, the abstract of\n\\citet{SienickiSienicki2026}, the comparator README, the nanoda README,\nand the manuscript extract. The script searches each for the words such\na statement would be likely to use (``practical'', ``brute'', ``trying\nall'', ``exhaustive'', ``$2^n$'', ``constants are'', ``concrete''). Outside\nthe manuscript, none occurs. In the manuscript, ``practical'' occurs once\n(page 3, line 153), in a sentence about the practical value of SAT\nsolvers on industrial formulas, a different sense. ``Exhaustive'' occurs\neleven times, as the motivation of SETH quoted above (line 170) and as the\nproof's own fallback for small inputs (lines 2480 and 2487, where the\nalgorithm uses ``exhaustive search'' below the cutoff\n$n \\ge 20$). The nearest earlier statements are the manuscript's own\nsentence that Lean verifies ``the weaker exponent'' (page 2, lines\n63--67; Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) and the scope note: both say the\nverified exponent is the weaker one, and neither says at what sizes the\nverified bound exceeds $2^n$, which is the title's point. Within these\ntexts we found no prior statement of it. The search is bounded by that\nlist: we did not search the literature at large, the working group's\noutput, or the press.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:related-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing about the working group's findings.} The\n  collection's log says the group ``will examine and explain the\n  proof''; we hold nothing it has written, and we neither anticipate\n  nor contradict it.\n\\item \\textbf{The human audit does not cover this family.} It is cited\n  for its method and its stated lesson about PDF extraction; its body\n  was not fetched, and no sentence of it beyond the abstract is quoted.\n\\item \\textbf{The two hypothesis papers were not read.} They are cited\n  as the manuscript cites them, and the hypotheses are stated as the\n  manuscript states them (Section~\\ref{sec:consequences}).\n\\item \\textbf{We did not run the comparator.} What we reproduced is\n  stated in Section~\\ref{sec:proof}'s Table~\\ref{tab:proof-trust};\n  the comparator's statement match by exported declarations is not among\n  it.\n\\item \\textbf{No claim that landrun, lean4export or nanoda is correct.}\n  Each is quoted for what its README says it does; the replay's\n  evidence is that two independent checkers agreed on this export, and\n  the trust left in \\texttt{lean4export} is stated in\n  Section~\\ref{sec:replay}.\n\\item \\textbf{``No prior statement'' is bounded by the list above.} Six\n  texts, read with a script; not a literature search.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\endgroup\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":274,"oldLines":8,"newStart":274,"newLines":8,"lines":[" algorithm uses ``exhaustive search'' below the cutoff"," $n \\ge 20$). The nearest earlier statements are the manuscript's own"," sentence that Lean verifies ``the weaker exponent'' (page 2, lines","-63--67; Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) and the scope note: both say the","-verified exponent is the weaker one, and neither says at what sizes the","+63--67; Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}) and the scope note. Both say the","+verified exponent is the weaker one. Neither says at what sizes the"," verified bound exceeds $2^n$, which is the title's point. Within these"," texts we found no prior statement of it. The search is bounded by that"," list: we did not search the literature at large, the working group's"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01YGyffYUYNq95WRKp5U1q5d","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
