{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ca9Pkd2iC11BjBpcxypXgD","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec10_conclusion.tex has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17474a62-7527-453d-99c4-703178c27619","uuid":"456fcb7b-fed8-49d2-92ee-3b3fdf77047d","timestamp":"2026-11-05T09:20:06.458Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper3/latex/sec10_conclusion.tex","oldString":"changes (Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}). The two facts are true at\nonce, and neither diminishes the other: in the limit the exponent is\nsmaller than the number of variables by any factor one names, which is\na theorem in our own Lean (Section~\\ref{sec:lean}); on every size anyone\nwill write down, it is larger.","newString":"changes (Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}). Two facts are true at\nonce, and neither diminishes the other. In the limit, the exponent is\nsmaller than the number of variables by any factor one names; that is\na theorem in our own Lean (Section~\\ref{sec:lean}). On every size anyone\nwill write down, it is larger.","originalFile":"% Conclusion -- drafted by loop0015 item 09 (2026-11-05), in plain English\n% (owner's rule of 2026-11-05 05:30). The second subsection reviews every\n% entry of paper3/ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD written up to this section (S1-S8,\n% 01a-08xd, and 09a-09d of this item) and lists the ones that affect the\n% text as open, by entry number; the owner resolves an entry by editing the\n% paper and striking it. Nothing here is a new number: every figure quoted\n% is a later section's, with its section named.\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\\begingroup\\sloppy\n\nOpenAI's family 378 holds a theorem the Lean kernel accepts, and the\ntheorem refutes the Exponential Time Hypothesis as stated. We read it,\ninspected its proof, rebuilt the proof from source and re-checked it\nwith a second checker, and found no flaw. That is the first thing this\npaper says, and it is said without qualification. The second thing is\nwhat the title says, and it is a reading of the statement, not of the\nproof. The statement is a guarantee: a machine exists and halts within\na bound. The bound's constants exist and are never computed; where the proof\nwrites values down, its construction has at least 257 for the\nexponent's constant, and the theorem it exhibits has at least 258\nthere and at least 28,701 in front (Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and\n\\ref{sec:proof}). The exponent is the manuscript's weaker one, and with\nits constant set to one it exceeds the number of variables at every\nsize from 50 up to a number with 2,391 digits\n(Section~\\ref{sec:statement}). So at every practical size the verified\nguarantee is weaker than the guarantee of trying every assignment. The\nmanuscript's sharper exponents, which would change that sentence, are\nnot in the Lean library (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}). What follows from\nthe verified statement is exactly what the manuscript says follows: the\nhypotheses fall, the lower bounds derived from them lose their premise,\nand the conclusions of those lower bounds are untouched\n(Section~\\ref{sec:consequences}). For anyone who runs a solver, nothing\nchanges (Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}). The two facts are true at\nonce, and neither diminishes the other: in the limit the exponent is\nsmaller than the number of variables by any factor one names, which is\na theorem in our own Lean (Section~\\ref{sec:lean}); on every size anyone\nwill write down, it is larger.\n\n\\subsection{What is claimed, and what is not}\n\\label{sec:conclusion-claims}\n\nThe introduction's two lists (Section~\\ref{sec:intro-claims}) are the\npaper's claims and non-claims, and each later section repeats its own\nunder ``what this does not claim''. In one sentence each. The verified\nstatement is a guarantee with uncomputed constants, above $2^n$ at every\npractical size with the constants set to one, and it refutes ETH and its\nrelatives nonetheless. The proof shows no flaw by inspection, builds\nfrom source, and passes two checkers. The sharper exponents are\npaper-only. And no running time of any machine, no flaw in anything,\nno value of any constant beyond the proof's own, and no refutation of\nany derived lower bound's conclusion is asserted. Every number in the\npaper has a script under \\repofile{paper3/scripts/} that regenerates\nit, every quotation has a page and line or a file and line, and the\nreplay's logs and hashes are under \\repofile{paper3/replay/}.\n\n\\subsection{The questions this reading left open, by entry}\n\\label{sec:conclusion-open}\n\nThe paper was written under a rule that no question stops the work.\nA choice between two sound ways of saying or doing a thing is made and\nrecorded in \\repofile{paper3/ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD} as one numbered entry.\nThe entry says what was ambiguous, what was chosen and what the author\nmay want to change. Then the writing goes on. Sixty-one entries were\nwritten in all: forty-four before the first draft of this section,\nfour by that draft, eleven by the reading of the whole paper and the\nwrite-up that followed it, and two by the final pass. We reviewed all\nsixty-one and sort them here. Twenty-two need no decision. Seven were\nresolved by the author in writing: S1, the author line; S3, where the\ncompanion Lean lives, with 07a following it; S4, no width references;\nS6, the model counters; S7, the phrase ``for every $50 \\le n <\ne^{5504}$''; S8, the replay's Lean version. Fifteen are procedural, or\nare corrections the text already carries: S5, the per-item time cap of\nthe writing driver; 01a, the sandbox's core cap; 03b, the manuscript\nhas 67 pages; 04b, 04d and 04e, three counts corrected from an earlier\naudit; 06b, a counter installed for Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner};\n08xb, which sections the plain-English rewrite covered; 10c, the long\nsentences the first reading left, since split; 11c, two audit files\nannotated rather than edited; 11d, the later entries listed in the\nrepository's README, and since here too; 11e, a paragraph for the\nrepository's instructions file, drafted and not applied; 11f, the\nplan's record of what was done; 12a and 12b, the scope of the final\npass and this list's own re-count. The thirty-nine below affect the\ntext and are open. In each case the text stands as chosen, and the\nentry says what would change.\n\n\\begin{description}\\setlength{\\itemsep}{1pt}\n\\item[The title and its figures.] S2: $2^n$ is typeset with a\n  superscript and the ellipsis as three dots; the plain text is in the\n  PDF's metadata. 02a: Section~\\ref{sec:statement} carries both the\n  two-panel Figure~\\ref{fig:central} and Figure~\\ref{fig:runtime-scale},\n  three drawings of one function. 02b: the central figure's peak is\n  placed analytically and its legend carries the $+16$. 10b: the\n  title's ``is slower'' is reconciled with the paper's non-claim of any\n  running time by Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and\n  \\ref{sec:practitioner}, and by nothing in the title itself.\n\\item[The statement (Section~\\ref{sec:statement}).] 02c: a proof-side\n  fact, the constant $256 + A_t$, is quoted in the statement section\n  for its value. 02d: the Lean theorem is transcribed as a display, not\n  shown verbatim, because the house build cannot typeset its symbols.\n  08xd: Section~\\ref{sec:statement} says the proof's constant is at\n  least 257 and the exhibited theorem's at least 258, two readings of\n  two lines, both kept.\n\\item[Beyond the statement (Section~\\ref{sec:beyond}).] 03a: a third\n  tag, ``supported'', between verified and paper-only. 03c: what the\n  verified exponent would give through Corollary~1.4's argument is not\n  worked out. 03d: Corollary~1.2 is stated with the verified exponent\n  and tagged supported, not verified.\n\\item[The proof and the replay (Sections~\\ref{sec:proof} and\n  \\ref{sec:replay}).] 01b: the second checker is \\texttt{nanoda\\_lib}\n  at the commit named, with \\texttt{lean4export} at the nearest tag.\n  01c: that checker was run with the three axioms permitted and unused\n  axioms ignored. 01d: ``replayed'' means the build, the axiom output\n  and the second checker, not the comparator run. 04a: thirty-five\n  definitions compared, thirty-three identical, two up to a keyword;\n  the audit's ``33'' is explained, not kept. 04c: the exhibited\n  constants, $A \\ge 258$ and $C \\ge 28{,}701$, are stated with their\n  lines.\n\\item[What follows (Section~\\ref{sec:consequences}).] 05a: the\n  manuscript's list of problems is elided from one quotation, by the\n  paper's scope. 05b: SETH is refuted by reading the statement at each\n  width, and ``SETH implies ETH'' is cited for the record. 05c: the\n  hypotheses' sources are copied from the manuscript's reference list\n  and not re-read. 05d: two paragraphs are ours and not the\n  manuscript's, and say so.\n\\item[For the practitioner (Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}).] 06a: the\n  two guarantee curves share one scale factor, so that they cross where\n  the statement puts the crossing. 06c: seven references for the tools\n  and the threshold were typed from memory; their DOIs have since been\n  checked (10a). 06d: the counters are reported at their defaults,\n  including a plateau that looks like a preprocessing budget.\n\\item[Our Lean (Section~\\ref{sec:lean}).] 07b: the axiom audit prints\n  two lines with fewer than the three axioms, and ``every line on the\n  three'' is read as ``nothing beyond the three''. 07c: the re-diff also\n  matches the restatement's body to the theorem's, beyond what was\n  asked.\n\\item[Related work (Section~\\ref{sec:related}).] 08a: the audit paper's\n  title is typeset with an apostrophe the arXiv record lacks. 08b: four\n  small public records were fetched beside the three allowed pages, for\n  their dates and commits. 08c: fourteen references carried no checked\n  DOI at the time; thirteen have since been found (10a). 08d: ``we\n  found no earlier statement'' is bounded to six texts.\n\\item[Plain English, and this section.] 08xa: the dense passages were\n  moved into Appendix~\\ref{app:tech}, not cut. 08xc: the glosses are\n  repeated in each section and now also in the introduction. 09a: the\n  introduction and abstract state the title's third clause in words,\n  with no formula beyond $2^n$, by the plain-English rule rather than\n  by the item's brief. 09b: the abstract carries two numbers, both from\n  Section~\\ref{sec:statement}'s script, and no count from the replay.\n  09c: this sorting of the entries into resolved, procedural and open\n  is the author's to redo. 09d: ``people who checked'' in the stance\n  sentence is read as the author and the assistant the acknowledgements\n  disclose.\n\\item[The write-up around the paper.] 10a: thirteen DOIs were added\n  from a registry the download rule did not name. 10d: the reading's\n  findings that were recorded and not fixed at the time; the two it\n  names have since been written and annotated. 11a: the data statement\n  takes its repository address and licence from our first paper. 11b:\n  the acknowledgements name the second reading as a second session of\n  the same assistant. 11g: the references for Lean~4 and Mathlib are\n  copied from our first paper's bibliography.\n\\end{description}\n\nFourteen of the thirty-nine are bibliographic or typographic: S2, 02b,\n02d, 05c, 06c, 08a, 08b, 08c, 11g, and the five spelling, placement and\ntagging choices of 02a, 03a, 03d, 04a, 08xa. The rest are choices\nabout what the text says. Each is a sentence that can be struck\nwithout touching any number.\n\n\\subsection{What this does not claim}\n\\label{sec:conclusion-not}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Nothing new.} This section states no fact that a\n  previous section does not state with its source; the numbers in it\n  are Sections~\\ref{sec:statement}, \\ref{sec:proof} and\n  \\ref{sec:replay}'s.\n\\item \\textbf{The list of open questions is complete to the day it was\n  last re-counted}, 2026-11-05, and includes the entries of the reading\n  of the whole paper and of the final pass. Any entry added after that\n  day is in the file and not here.\n\\item \\textbf{No question is answered here.} Each entry records a\n  choice and what would change; the author's answer, where there is\n  one, is in the file's ``Owner resolutions'' and in the text.\n\\item \\textbf{No claim about the working group.} The collection says a\n  group of computer scientists will examine the proof\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:related}); nothing of theirs is on record here, and\n  their reading of the proof will carry more weight on the proof than\n  our inspection does.\n\\end{itemize}\n\\par\\endgroup\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":31,"oldLines":10,"newStart":31,"newLines":10,"lines":[" hypotheses fall, the lower bounds derived from them lose their premise,"," and the conclusions of those lower bounds are untouched"," (Section~\\ref{sec:consequences}). For anyone who runs a solver, nothing","-changes (Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}). The two facts are true at","-once, and neither diminishes the other: in the limit the exponent is","-smaller than the number of variables by any factor one names, which is","-a theorem in our own Lean (Section~\\ref{sec:lean}); on every size anyone","+changes (Section~\\ref{sec:practitioner}). Two facts are true at","+once, and neither diminishes the other. In the limit, the exponent is","+smaller than the number of variables by any factor one names; that is","+a theorem in our own Lean (Section~\\ref{sec:lean}). On every size anyone"," will write down, it is larger."," "," \\subsection{What is claimed, and what is not}"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Ca9Pkd2iC11BjBpcxypXgD","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
