{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015SSPowaCR9RtdwpPmL4xJa","type":"tool_result","content":"    40\tallowed and unsatisfiable, a tautological clause \\(\\{x, \\neg x\\}\\) is\n    41\tallowed and counts two toward the width. That is consistent with the\n    42\tmanuscript's ``Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than \\(k\\)\n    43\tliterals'' (p.~1, line~44).\n    44\t\n    45\t\\paragraph{The input encoding (lines 53--81).}\n    46\t\\texttt{encodeNat n} is \\(n\\) ones followed by a zero (line 55): unary.\n    47\t\\texttt{frame} doubles a bit string, prefixing each bit with a one, and\n    48\tends it with a zero (lines 57--58). \\texttt{encodeList enc} prefixes each\n    49\telement's frame with a one and ends the list with a zero (lines 60--62).\n    50\tA literal becomes the number \\(2\\cdot\\mathrm{var} + [\\text{positive}]\\),\n    51\tat most \\(2n - 1\\) (lines 64--65); a clause is its sorted list of literal\n    52\tnumbers, a formula its sorted list of clauses (lines 67--71).\n    53\t\\texttt{encodeCNF F} is \\texttt{encodeNat n} followed by the formula's\n    54\tlist of lists of unary numbers (lines 73--77), and\n    55\t\\texttt{formulaBitLength F} is the length of that bit string (line 79).\n    56\tTwo things follow. The number of variables \\(n\\) is part of the input,\n    57\tso a variable that occurs in no clause is still counted. And the length\n    58\tis polynomial: a clause of \\(j \\le k\\) literals costs \\(2j(4n+1)+4\\) bits,\n    59\tso\n    60\t\\[\n    61\t  L \\;=\\; \\mathtt{formulaBitLength}\\ F \\;\\le\\; n + 2 + m\\,\\bigl(2k(4n+1) + 4\\bigr)\n    62\t  \\;=\\; O(k\\,n\\,m)\n    63\t\\]\n    64\tfor \\(m\\) clauses. The solution itself proves\n    65\t\\((L+1)^d \\le (8k+7)^d\\,(n + |F| + 1)^{2d}\\)\n    66\t(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Statement.lean}, lines 9--14). Emulating the\n    67\tdefinitions (\\repofile{reports/scratch/eth_audit/check_encoding.py},\n    68\trandom seed 1), one random formula with \\(n = 125\\), \\(m = 500\\),\n    69\t\\(k = 4\\) encodes to \\(L = 1{,}006{,}223\\) bits and one with\n    70\t\\(n = 1{,}000\\), \\(m = 4{,}000\\), \\(k = 3\\) to \\(47.9\\) million, both\n    71\tunder the bound; the length depends on the formula's literals, not on\n    72\t\\((n, m, k)\\) alone. Nothing in the\n    73\tencoding is exponential; the unary numbers are linear in \\(n\\).\n    74\t\n    75\t\\paragraph{The machine (lines 83--128).}\n    76\t\\texttt{PrimitiveMachine} (lines 92--99) is a finite object: a number\n    77\t\\texttt{tapeCount} of tapes and a number \\texttt{stateCount} of states,\n    78\tan input tape and a start state, a table \\texttt{scan} saying which tape\n    79\teach state reads, and a table \\texttt{transition} from\n    80\t\\(\\mathtt{Fin}\\ \\mathtt{stateCount} \\times \\mathtt{Option\\ Bool}\\)---a\n    81\tdomain of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) points---into either a halting\n    82\tanswer (a \\texttt{Bool}) or a next state with one action. The action type\n    83\tis Mathlib's \\texttt{Turing.TM0.Stmt (Option Bool)}: move the head left or\n    84\tright, or write one of the three symbols \\texttt{none}, \\texttt{some\n    85\tfalse}, \\texttt{some true}, five values in all\n    86\t(\\path{Mathlib/Computability/TuringMachine/PostTuringMachine.lean}\n    87\tlines 118--120 and \\path{Tape.lean} lines 421--423 at Mathlib\n    88\t\\texttt{d13f23b7}, the clone's pinned revision). A configuration\n    89\t\\texttt{Cfg} (lines 103--105) is a state, halted or not, and one Mathlib\n    90\t\\texttt{Tape} per tape index (a head symbol and two blank-padded halves,\n    91\t\\path{Tape.lean} lines 408--414, blank \\(=\\) \\texttt{none}).\n    92\t\\texttt{step} (lines 107--119) returns \\texttt{none} in a halted state;\n    93\totherwise it reads the head of the one tape the state scans, looks the\n    94\tpair up in the table, and either halts with the answer, or writes one\n    95\tsymbol or moves that one head by one cell and changes state. \\texttt{init}\n    96\t(lines 121--124) puts the input on the input tape with the head at its\n    97\tfirst bit and every other tape blank (\\texttt{Tape.mk}$_1$,\n    98\t\\path{Tape.lean} line 494). A Lean function on a finite domain is,\n    99\twhatever term defines it, a finite table; so a step is one lookup in a\n   100\ttable of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) rows, and no field of the\n   101\tstructure can carry unbounded computation.\n   102\t\n   103\t\\paragraph{Traces (lines 87--90).}\n   104\t\\texttt{Trace step t a c} is an inductive proposition with two\n   105\tconstructors: \\texttt{refl}, a trace of length 0 from \\(a\\) to \\(a\\)\n   106\titself, and \\texttt{cons}, which extends a trace from \\(b\\) by one\n   107\tsuccessful step \\(a \\mapsto b\\). So it holds exactly when \\(c\\) is\n   108\treached from \\(a\\) by \\(t\\) successful applications of \\texttt{step}, and\n   109\ta trace of length 0 to a different configuration is impossible. Since\n   110\t\\texttt{step} is a function, the run is deterministic.\n   111\t\n   112\t\\paragraph{Space monitoring (lines 147--189).}\n   113\t\\texttt{LocatedCfg} (lines 149--153) pairs a configuration with, per tape,\n   114\tthe head's current position and the least and greatest positions seen so\n   115\tfar, as integers; \\texttt{locatedInit} starts every head at 0 with the\n   116\tinput tape's range covering the input (lines 155--159);\n   117\t\\texttt{headDisplacement} is \\(-1\\), \\(+1\\) or \\(0\\) for a move left, a\n   118\tmove right or a write (lines 161--164); \\texttt{monitor} (lines 166--182)\n   119\tapplies the same table lookup to update the position and the running\n   120\tminimum and maximum of the scanned tape, and nothing else;\n   121\t\\texttt{locatedStep} is \\texttt{step} on the configuration followed by\n   122\t\\texttt{monitor} (lines 184--186). \\texttt{visitedSpace} (lines 188--189)\n   123\tis the sum over tapes of \\(\\mathrm{upper} - \\mathrm{lower} + 1\\): the\n   124\tnumber of cells whose head position was ever visited. \\texttt{countWord a}\n   125\tis the binary expansion of \\(a\\), least significant bit first, with\n   126\t\\texttt{[false]} for \\(0\\) (line 147), and \\texttt{toNat} (lines 7--9)\n   127\tdecodes such a word.\n   128\t\n   129\t\\paragraph{The exponent and the bound (lines 130--141).}\n   130\t\\begin{quote}\\small","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17474a62-7527-453d-99c4-703178c27619","uuid":"d7c7283b-fbd7-448d-8b0b-907213f4c36b","timestamp":"2026-11-05T09:20:13.405Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"    40\tallowed and unsatisfiable, a tautological clause \\(\\{x, \\neg x\\}\\) is\n    41\tallowed and counts two toward the width. That is consistent with the\n    42\tmanuscript's ``Repeating a literal permits clauses with fewer than \\(k\\)\n    43\tliterals'' (p.~1, line~44).\n    44\t\n    45\t\\paragraph{The input encoding (lines 53--81).}\n    46\t\\texttt{encodeNat n} is \\(n\\) ones followed by a zero (line 55): unary.\n    47\t\\texttt{frame} doubles a bit string, prefixing each bit with a one, and\n    48\tends it with a zero (lines 57--58). \\texttt{encodeList enc} prefixes each\n    49\telement's frame with a one and ends the list with a zero (lines 60--62).\n    50\tA literal becomes the number \\(2\\cdot\\mathrm{var} + [\\text{positive}]\\),\n    51\tat most \\(2n - 1\\) (lines 64--65); a clause is its sorted list of literal\n    52\tnumbers, a formula its sorted list of clauses (lines 67--71).\n    53\t\\texttt{encodeCNF F} is \\texttt{encodeNat n} followed by the formula's\n    54\tlist of lists of unary numbers (lines 73--77), and\n    55\t\\texttt{formulaBitLength F} is the length of that bit string (line 79).\n    56\tTwo things follow. The number of variables \\(n\\) is part of the input,\n    57\tso a variable that occurs in no clause is still counted. And the length\n    58\tis polynomial: a clause of \\(