ail behind Section~\\ref{sec:consequences-hypotheses}.\n   388\tThe property used throughout: for each fixed $k \\ge 2$ there is one\n   389\tdeterministic machine which, on the canonical encoding of any\n   390\t\\texttt{WidthAtMost}~$k$ formula $F$ on $n$ variables (comparator line\n   391\t38), halts with \\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape (lines\n   392\t203--204, 147), the bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ on its answer tape and\n   393\tin its halting state (lines 205--207), within $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$\n   394\tsteps (lines 210--211). Since $\\rhon/n \\to 0$ \\leanref{rho-little-o},\n   395\tfor every $\\delta > 0$ there is an $n_0(k, \\delta)$ beyond which the\n   396\tbound is below $2^{\\delta n}$; below $n_0$ the bound is a constant\n   397\tdepending on $k$ and $\\delta$. So the running time is\n   398\t$O_{k,\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})$ for every $\\delta > 0$, and\n   399\t$2^{\\delta n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$ with the polynomial written out.\n   400\t\n   401\t\\paragraph{ETH.} In quantifiers: there is a $\\delta > 0$ such that no\n   402\tdeterministic algorithm decides 3-SAT within $2^{\\delta n}\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$\n   403\tsteps on all inputs. Its negation: for every $\\delta > 0$ there is one.\n   404\tThe verified statement at $k = 3$ supplies, for every $\\delta > 0$, the\n   405\tsame machine, whose answer bit is the decision, within\n   406\t$O_{\\delta}(2^{\\delta n})\\,\\mathrm{poly}(n+m)$ steps. This holds on every\n   407\tinput of every size, so it refutes the hypothesis under either reading\n   408\tof ``$2^{\\Omega(n)}$'' (a lower bound at infinitely many $n$, or at all\n   409\tlarge $n$). In the vocabulary of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturi2001}, where\n   410\t$s_k$ is the infimum of the $\\delta$ for which $k$-SAT has an\n   411\t$O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm and ETH is $s_3 > 0$: the verified statement\n   412\tgives $s_k = 0$ for every $k \\ge 2$, by one algorithm per $k$.\n   413\t\n   414\t\\paragraph{SETH.} The manuscript's form: for every\n   415\t$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$, there is a fixed $k$ for which no deterministic\n   416\talgorithm solves $k$-SAT in $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ time. The\n   417\tnegation: there is an $\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ such that for every fixed\n   418\t$k$ there is a deterministic $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$\n   419\talgorithm. The verified statement gives more: for every\n   420\t$\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$ and every $k \\ge 2$, the machine for $k$ runs\n   421\twithin $C_k(L+1)^{d_k}\\,2^{A_k\\rhon}$ steps, and\n   422\t$A_k\\rhon \\le (1-\\varepsilon)n$ for all $n \\ge n_0(k, \\varepsilon)$\n   423\tbecause $\\rhon = o(n)$; below $n_0$ the exponential factor is a\n   424\tconstant. So each $k$ has a $2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O_k(1)}$\n   425\talgorithm, and the polynomial's degree depending on $k$ is exactly what\n   426\tthe hypothesis's ``for a fixed $k$'' permits. For the record, SETH\n   427\timplies ETH: if 3-SAT had a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm then, by the\n   428\tSparsification Lemma of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}, which reduces\n   429\t$k$-SAT on $n$ variables to $2^{\\varepsilon n}$ instances with\n   430\t$O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ clauses and so, after the clause-splitting\n   431\treduction, to 3-SAT on $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ variables, so would\n   432\t$k$-SAT for every fixed $k$.\n   433\t\n   434\t\\paragraph{Gap-ETH.} The negation of the manuscript's form: for every\n   435\t$\\delta, \\gamma > 0$ there is an $O(2^{\\gamma n})$ algorithm that\n   436\tseparates a satisfiable 3-CNF from one in which every assignment\n   437\tviolates at least a $\\delta$ fraction of the clauses, already at\n   438\t$m = O(n)$. Exact decision separates them, since a formula in which\n   439\tevery assignment violates a positive fraction of the clauses is\n   440\tunsatisfiable; and the verified machine at $k = 3$ decides within\n   441\t$n^{O(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon} = O_{\\gamma}(2^{\\gamma n})$ steps for every\n   442\t$\\gamma > 0$, at every $m$ (at $m = O(n)$ the polynomial is\n   443\t$O(n^{2d})$). The promise is never used: the machine answers correctly\n   444\ton every input, promised or not, with probability $1$, which refutes\n   445\tthe randomised form as well.\n   446\t\n   447\t\\paragraph{NSETH.} The negation of the manuscript's form: there is an\n   448\t$\\varepsilon > 0$ such that every fixed $k$ has a nondeterministic\n   449\t$2^{(1-\\varepsilon)n}(n+m)^{O(1)}$ algorithm for unsatisfiability. The\n   450\tverified machine's answer bit is \\texttt{false} exactly when\n   451\t$\\mathtt{count}\\ F = 0$ (lines 205--207), so it decides unsatisfiability\n   452\tdeterministically within the SETH bound above for every $\\varepsilon$\n   453\tand every $k$; and a deterministic machine is a nondeterministic one\n   454\twhose only computation path is the deterministic run, so every\n   455\tacceptance convention for nondeterministic time is met.\n   456\t\n   457\t\\paragraph{\\#ETH.} The negation: for every $\\delta > 0$ there is a\n   458\tdeterministic $O(2^{\\delta n})$ algorithm that outputs the exact count.\n   459\tThe verified machine at $k = 3$ halts with \\texttt{countWord (count F)}\n   460\ton its count tape, a binary word of length at most $n + 1$ that decodes\n   461\tto $\\mathtt{count}\\ F$ (lines 203--204, 208--209), within the same bound.\n   462\t\n   463\t\\paragraph{Shape (S) in detail.} For a theorem ``SETH $\\Rightarrow$ no\n   464\t$(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$'', a $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm\n   465\tfor $Y$ obtained from the verified statement would need the exponent of\n   466\tthe SAT instance to be below $k \\log_2 c$ by a constant factor, and the\n   467\tverified statement's exponent is $A\\rho(n')$ in the variable count $n'$\n   468\tof the SAT instance with $A$ unknown; no value of $A$ is on record below\n   469\t$257$, and no base $c$ is derived from the verified statement for any\n   470\t$Y$.\n   471\t\n   472\t\\subsection{How the two guara