llion, both\nunder the bound; the length depends on the formula's literals, not on\n\\((n, m, k)\\) alone. Nothing in the\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"random seed 1), one random formula with \\(n = 125\\), \\(m = 500\\),\n\\(k = 4\\) encodes to \\(L = 1{,}006{,}223\\) bits, and one with\n\\(n = 1{,}000\\), \\(m = 4{,}000\\), \\(k = 3\\) to \\(47.9\\) million. Both are\nunder the bound. The length depends on the formula's literals, not on\n\\((n, m, k)\\) alone. Nothing in the\"\"\"),\n# B.1 machine\n(r\"\"\"\\texttt{PrimitiveMachine} (lines 92--99) is a finite object: a number\n\\texttt{tapeCount} of tapes and a number \\texttt{stateCount} of states,\nan input tape and a start state, a table \\texttt{scan} saying which tape\neach state reads, and a table \\texttt{transition} from\n\\(\\mathtt{Fin}\\ \\mathtt{stateCount} \\times \\mathtt{Option\\ Bool}\\)---a\ndomain of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) points---into either a halting\nanswer (a \\texttt{Bool}) or a next state with one action. The action type\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"\\texttt{PrimitiveMachine} (lines 92--99) is a finite object. It has a number\n\\texttt{tapeCount} of tapes and a number \\texttt{stateCount} of states,\nan input tape and a start state, and a table \\texttt{scan} saying which tape\neach state reads. Its table \\texttt{transition} maps\n\\(\\mathtt{Fin}\\ \\mathtt{stateCount} \\times \\mathtt{Option\\ Bool}\\)---a\ndomain of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) points---to either a halting\nanswer (a \\texttt{Bool}) or a next state with one action. The action type\"\"\"),\n(r\"\"\"\\texttt{step} (lines 107--119) returns \\texttt{none} in a halted state;\notherwise it reads the head of the one tape the state scans, looks the\npair up in the table, and either halts with the answer, or writes one\nsymbol or moves that one head by one cell and changes state. \\texttt{init}\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"In a halted state, \\texttt{step} (lines 107--119) returns \\texttt{none}.\nOtherwise it reads the head of the one tape the state scans and looks the\npair up in the table. Then it either halts with the answer, or writes one\nsymbol or moves that one head by one cell and changes state. \\texttt{init}\"\"\"),\n(r\"\"\"\\path{Tape.lean} line 494). A Lean function on a finite domain is,\nwhatever term defines it, a finite table; so a step is one lookup in a\ntable of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) rows, and no field of the\nstructure can carry unbounded computation.\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"\\path{Tape.lean} line 494). A Lean function on a finite domain is,\nwhatever term defines it, a finite table. So a step is one lookup in a\ntable of \\(3 \\cdot \\mathtt{stateCount}\\) rows, and no field of the\nstructure can carry unbounded computation.\"\"\"),\n# B.1 space monitoring\n(r\"\"\"\\texttt{LocatedCfg} (lines 149--153) pairs a configuration with, per tape,\nthe head's current position and the least and greatest positions seen so\nfar, as integers; \\texttt{locatedInit} starts every head at 0 with the\ninput tape's range covering the input (lines 155--159);\n\\texttt{headDisplacement} is \\(-1\\), \\(+1\\) or \\(0\\) for a move left, a\nmove right or a write (lines 161--164); \\texttt{monitor} (lines 166--182)\napplies the same table lookup to update the position and the running\nminimum and maximum of the scanned tape, and nothing else;\n\\texttt{locatedStep} is \\texttt{step} on the configuration followed by\n\\texttt{monitor} (lines 184--186). \\texttt{visitedSpace} (lines 188--189)\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"\\texttt{LocatedCfg} (lines 149--153) pairs a configuration with, per tape,\nthe head's current position and the least and greatest positions seen so\nfar, as integers. \\texttt{locatedInit} starts every head at 0 with the\ninput tape's range covering the input (lines 155--159).\n\\texttt{headDisplacement} is \\(-1\\), \\(+1\\) or \\(0\\) for a move left, a\nmove right or a write (lines 161--164). \\texttt{monitor} (lines 166--182)\napplies the same table lookup to update the position and the running\nminimum and maximum of the scanned tape, and nothing else.\n\\texttt{locatedStep} is \\texttt{step} on the configuration followed by\n\\texttt{monitor} (lines 184--186). \\texttt{visitedSpace} (lines 188--189)\"\"\"),\n# B.2 theorem\n(r\"\"\"with no explicit function of \\(k\\). There is no lower bound on \\(n\\): the\nmanuscript's Theorem~1.1 and Proposition~6.4 are stated for \\(n \\ge 20\\)\n(p.~2, lines 56--57; p.~43, lines 2474--2475), and the solution's\nauxiliary \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} keeps \\(20 \\le n\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 104--109); the exported\ntheorem does not, and the \\(+16\\) in \\(\\rhon\\) is what makes that\npossible.\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"with no explicit function of \\(k\\). There is no lower bound on \\(n\\). The\nmanuscript's Theorem~1.1 and Proposition~6.4 are stated for \\(n \\ge 20\\)\n(p.~2, lines 56--57; p.~43, lines 2474--2475), and the solution's\nauxiliary \\texttt{exact\\_count\\_eth} keeps \\(20 \\le n\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, lines 104--109). The exported\ntheorem does not, and the \\(+16\\) in \\(\\rhon\\) is what makes that\npossible.\"\"\"),\n(r\"\"\"The proof's own constants, read for their values only: at the point where\nthe large-input run is assembled, the exponent constant is set to\n\\(A = 256 + A_t\\) with \\(A_t \\ge 1\\) taken from a leaf contract\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 51;\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean}, lines 792--793), and\n\\(1 \\le C\\), \\(1 \\le A\\) are in the large-input run's own statement\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 12). The witness\nmachine is compiled from a program over 80 one-bit registers and 1,220\ntapes, with \\(\\mathtt{countTape} = 256\\) and \\(\\mathtt{answerTape} = 1216\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}: the program's type at line 11, the\nmachine built from it at lines 98--101); its state count is\na finite type's cardinality that nothing computes. The solution's\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"The proof's own constants, read for their values only. At the point where\nthe large-input run is assembled, the exponent constant is set to\n\\(A = 256 + A_t\\) with \\(A_t \\ge 1\\) taken from a leaf contract\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 51;\n\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean}, lines 792--793). And\n\\(1 \\le C\\), \\(1 \\le A\\) are in the large-input run's own statement\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLargeEntry.lean}, line 12). The witness\nmachine is compiled from a program over 80 one-bit registers and 1,220\ntapes, with \\(\\mathtt{countTape} = 256\\) and \\(\\mathtt{answerTape} = 1216\\)\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}: the program's type at line 11, the\nmachine built from it at lines 98--101). Its state count is\na finite type's cardinality that nothing computes. The solution's\"\"\"),\n(r\"\"\"The clause-count bound used in Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and\n\\ref{sec:consequences}: a \\texttt{Finset} of clauses of width at most\n\\(k\\) over \\(n\\) variables has at most \\(\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j} =\nO(n^k)\\) members (\\(2{,}604{,}376\\) at \\(n = 125\\), \\(k = 3\\)), so\n\\(m = O(n^k)\\), \\(L = O(k\\,n\\,m) = O(n^{k+1})\\), and the whole bound is\n\\(n^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\), which is \\(2^{o(n)}\\) in \\(n\\) alone because\n\\(\\rhon/n \\to 0\\) \\leanref{rho-little-o}.\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"The clause-count bound used in Sections~\\ref{sec:statement} and\n\\ref{sec:consequences} is this. A \\texttt{Finset} of clauses of width at most\n\\(k\\) over \\(n\\) variables has at most \\(\\sum_{j \\le k}\\binom{2n}{j} =\nO(n^k)\\) members (\\(2{,}604{,}376\\) at \\(n = 125\\), \\(k = 3\\)). So\n\\(m = O(n^k)\\), \\(L = O(k\\,n\\,m) = O(n^{k+1})\\), and the whole bound is\n\\(n^{O_k(1)}\\,2^{A\\rhon}\\). That is \\(2^{o(n)}\\) in \\(n\\) alone, because\n\\(\\rhon/n \\to 0\\) \\leanref{rho-little-o}.\"\"\"),\n# B.3 spine\n(r\"\"\"\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~100), where \\texttt{program}\nis \\texttt{Native.countingProgram cutoff k} \\texttt{(NativeLeafProgram.command k)}\n(line~40), a \\texttt{BitProgram.Program 80 1220 labels} (line~11): a\nfirst-order program over 80 one-bit registers, 1,220 Boolean stacks and\na finite label set, with twelve instruction kinds\n(\\clonefile{BitProgram.lean}, lines 10--26). \\texttt{primitive}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~131) is a\n\\texttt{noncomputable abbrev}: it takes the transition table\n\\texttt{transition p} (lines 30--47, one case per instruction) and the\nstack selector \\texttt{selected p} (lines 14--17), feeds them to the\nstack compiler's controller, and renames the finite state type to\n\\texttt{Fin stateCount} through \\texttt{Fintype.equivFin} (line~132).\nThe machine is thus a concrete table defined by pattern matching on the\nprogram, composed with a classical renaming of states; the theorem is\nexistential, so the noncomputability costs it nothing.\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~100). Here \\texttt{program}\nis \\texttt{Native.countingProgram cutoff k} \\texttt{(NativeLeafProgram.command k)}\n(line~40), of type \\texttt{BitProgram.Program 80 1220 labels} (line~11). That is a\nfirst-order program over 80 one-bit registers, 1,220 Boolean stacks and\na finite label set, with twelve instruction kinds\n(\\clonefile{BitProgram.lean}, lines 10--26). \\texttt{primitive}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~131) is a\n\\texttt{noncomputable abbrev}. It takes the transition table\n\\texttt{transition p} (lines 30--47, one case per instruction) and the\nstack selector \\texttt{selected p} (lines 14--17), feeds them to the\nstack compiler's controller, and renames the finite state type to\n\\texttt{Fin stateCount} through \\texttt{Fintype.equivFin} (line~132).\nThe machine is thus a concrete table defined by pattern matching on the\nprogram, composed with a classical renaming of states. The theorem is\nexistential, so the noncomputability costs it nothing.\"\"\"),\n(r\"\"\"gives $1 \\le \\mathtt{At}$, \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean},\nline~793), and its cutoff is \\texttt{max 20 (max Nleaf Ns)} (line~50),\nwith \\texttt{Ns} obtained non-constructively from an eventual inequality\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"gives $1 \\le \\mathtt{At}$, \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeLeafContract.lean},\nline~793). Its cutoff is \\texttt{max 20 (max Nleaf Ns)} (line~50),\nwith \\texttt{Ns} obtained non-constructively from an eventual inequality\"\"\"),\n(r\"\"\"(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuterBudgets.lean}, lines 297 and 317)\ninto one triple, and the witnesses the final theorem exhibits are\n\\texttt{C + 28700, A + 1, S + 5022741, d + 2, e + 2}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~41). So the proof as written\nhas an exponent constant of at least $258$ in front of $\\rhon$, a\npolynomial degree \\texttt{d} that depends on $k$ (line~51: $\\mathtt{db} + (4(k-2)+2) + \\mathtt{df} + 2$) and a leading constant of at least\n$28{,}701$; none of these is in the statement, which asserts only that\nthey are positive, and none of \\texttt{At}, \\texttt{db}, \\texttt{df} is\ncomputed anywhere.\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuterBudgets.lean}, lines 297 and 317)\ninto one triple. The witnesses the final theorem exhibits are\n\\texttt{C + 28700, A + 1, S + 5022741, d + 2, e + 2}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}, line~41). So the proof as written\nhas an exponent constant of at least $258$ in front of $\\rhon$, a\npolynomial degree \\texttt{d} that depends on $k$ (line~51: $\\mathtt{db} + (4(k-2)+2) + \\mathtt{df} + 2$) and a leading constant of at least\n$28{,}701$. None of these is in the statement, which asserts only that\nthey are positive. None of \\texttt{At}, \\texttt{db}, \\texttt{df} is\ncomputed anywhere.\"\"\"),\n(r\"\"\"The compile lemmas conclude in\nthe comparator's own vocabulary: \\path{GuardedCompiler.primitive_execution}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, lines 160--162) turns a\nsource run of \\texttt{n} steps into \\texttt{RunsIn (primitive p).step\n(7 * n)}, with the overhead \\texttt{7} proved as a theorem\n(\\texttt{compiler\\_overhead}, lines 152--153), and\n\\path{CompileSpace.compile_word_answer_space}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/CompileSpace.lean}, lines 125--143) produces a\n\\texttt{Computation.Trace (locatedStep (primitive p)) time \\ldots} with\n$\\mathtt{time} \\le 7\\cdot\\mathtt{sourceSteps}$ and\n$\\mathtt{visitedSpace}\\ \\mathtt{intermediate} \\le s\\,(\\mathtt{bound} + \\mathtt{input.length} + 15)$ on\nevery prefix (lines 134, 135 and 143); \\path{primitive_located_step}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~600) is the per-step\nform.\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"The compile lemmas conclude in\nthe comparator's own vocabulary. \\path{GuardedCompiler.primitive_execution}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, lines 160--162) turns a\nsource run of \\texttt{n} steps into \\texttt{RunsIn (primitive p).step\n(7 * n)}, with the overhead \\texttt{7} proved as a theorem\n(\\texttt{compiler\\_overhead}, lines 152--153).\n\\path{CompileSpace.compile_word_answer_space}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/CompileSpace.lean}, lines 125--143) produces a\n\\texttt{Computation.Trace (locatedStep (primitive p)) time \\ldots} with\n$\\mathtt{time} \\le 7\\cdot\\mathtt{sourceSteps}$ and\n$\\mathtt{visitedSpace}\\ \\mathtt{intermediate} \\le s\\,(\\mathtt{bound} + \\mathtt{input.length} + 15)$ on\nevery prefix (lines 134, 135 and 143). \\path{primitive_located_step}\n(\\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean}, line~600) is the per-step\nform.\"\"\"),\n(r\"\"\"\\texttt{final.stacks ABI.answerStack = [decide (0 < SAT.count F)]} from\nthe canonical DFS execution, and\n\\path{Native.counting_primitive_from_entry} (line~73) transports\nboth to the primitive machine's tapes; \\texttt{countWord\\_toNat} and\n\\texttt{countWord\\_count\\_length} supply the decoding and length clauses\nof the statement.\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"\\texttt{final.stacks ABI.answerStack = [decide (0 < SAT.count F)]} from\nthe canonical DFS execution.\n\\path{Native.counting_primitive_from_entry} (line~73) transports\nboth to the primitive machine's tapes. \\texttt{countWord\\_toNat} and\n\\texttt{countWord\\_count\\_length} supply the decoding and length clauses\nof the statement.\"\"\"),\n(r\"\"\"and \\texttt{SqrtLog.StatementCanonical}. The library re-declares every\ndefinition the statement uses under the same fully qualified names, in\nseven files: the SAT vocabulary in \\clonefile{SAT.lean}, the bit\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"and \\texttt{SqrtLog.StatementCanonical}. The library re-declares every\ndefinition the statement uses under the same fully qualified names, in\nseven files. These are: the SAT vocabulary in \\clonefile{SAT.lean}, the bit\"\"\"),\n(r\"\"\"(line~17, the challenge's line~147). The definition diff compares 35\ndeclarations: 33 are identical character for character after whitespace\nnormalisation, and the remaining two, \\texttt{runtimeScale} and\n\\texttt{runtimeBound}, differ only in that the challenge writes\n\\texttt{noncomputable def} where the library opens a\n\\texttt{noncomputable section} (\\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean},\nline~15). The theorem statement itself, from \\texttt{theorem\nexact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} to \\texttt{:= by}, is identical in the two files,\n1,353 characters raw and 1,124 after normalisation, at line~194 of the\nchallenge and line~74 of \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}; the\nchallenge's proof is the single word \\texttt{sorry} at its line~218.\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"(line~17, the challenge's line~147). The definition diff compares 35\ndeclarations. 33 are identical character for character after whitespace\nnormalisation. The remaining two, \\texttt{runtimeScale} and\n\\texttt{runtimeBound}, differ only in that the challenge writes\n\\texttt{noncomputable def} where the library opens a\n\\texttt{noncomputable section} (\\clonefile{SqrtLog/StatementScale.lean},\nline~15). The theorem statement itself, from \\texttt{theorem\nexact\\_count\\_and\\_sat} to \\texttt{:= by}, is identical in the two files:\n1,353 characters raw and 1,124 after normalisation. It is at line~194 of the\nchallenge and line~74 of \\clonefile{SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean}. The\nchallenge's proof is the single word \\texttt{sorry} at its line~218.\"\"\"),\n# B.4 keywords\n(r\"\"\"which switch off a pre-check on local notation and have no bearing on\nsoundness; there is no \\texttt{maxHeartbeats}, \\texttt{maxRecDepth} or\n\\texttt{autoImplicit} option anywhere in the closure, and the lakefile\nsets \\texttt{autoImplicit} to \\texttt{false} for the whole package\n(\\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}, line~9). Nine declarations are\n\\texttt{opaque}, and every one carries a body: three recovery functions\n(\\texttt{queryRecovery} at line~59 of\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryQuery.lean}, with its\n\\texttt{:= by} at line~86; \\texttt{rawRecovery} and\n\\texttt{fixedRecovery} at lines 178 and 198 of\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryWitness.lean}) and six\n\\texttt{private opaque} constants of a subtype\n\\texttt{\\{ports // ports = \\ldots\\}}, each with an explicit value\n(line~22 of \\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramQueryCleanup.lean} and five\nothers). An \\texttt{opaque} with a body\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"which switch off a pre-check on local notation and have no bearing on\nsoundness. There is no \\texttt{maxHeartbeats}, \\texttt{maxRecDepth} or\n\\texttt{autoImplicit} option anywhere in the closure, and the lakefile\nsets \\texttt{autoImplicit} to \\texttt{false} for the whole package\n(\\clonefile{lean/lakefile.lean}, line~9). Nine declarations are\n\\texttt{opaque}, and every one carries a body. Three are recovery functions:\n\\texttt{queryRecovery} at line~59 of\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryQuery.lean}, with its\n\\texttt{:= by} at line~86, and \\texttt{rawRecovery} and\n\\texttt{fixedRecovery} at lines 178 and 198 of\n\\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramPreparedRecoveryWitness.lean}. Six are\n\\texttt{private opaque} constants of a subtype\n\\texttt{\\{ports // ports = \\ldots\\}}, each with an explicit value\n(line~22 of \\clonefile{SqrtLog/LeafProgramQueryCleanup.lean} and five\nothers). An \\texttt{opaque} with a body\"\"\"),\n# B.5 hypotheses\n(r\"\"\"The property used throughout: for each fixed $k \\ge 2$ there is one\ndeterministic machine which, on the canonical encoding of any\n\\texttt{WidthAtMost}~$k$ formula $F$ on $n$ variables (comparator line\n38), halts with \\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape (lines\n203--204, 147), the bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ on its answer tape and\nin its halting state (lines 205--207), within $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$\nsteps (lines 210--211). Since\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"The property used throughout is this. For each fixed $k \\ge 2$ there is one\ndeterministic machine. On the canonical encoding of any\n\\texttt{WidthAtMost}~$k$ formula $F$ on $n$ variables (comparator line\n38), it halts with \\texttt{countWord (count F)} on its count tape (lines\n203--204, 147) and the bit $[0 < \\mathtt{count}\\ F]$ on its answer tape and\nin its halting state (lines 205--207). It does so within $C\\,(L+1)^d\\,2^{A\\rhon}$\nsteps (lines 210--211). Since\"\"\"),\n(r\"\"\"the hypothesis's ``for a fixed $k$'' permits. For the record, SETH\nimplies ETH: if 3-SAT had a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm then, by the\nSparsification Lemma of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001}, which reduces\n$k$-SAT on $n$ variables to $2^{\\varepsilon n}$ instances with\n$O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ clauses and so, after the clause-splitting\nreduction, to 3-SAT on $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ variables, so would\n$k$-SAT for every fixed $k$.\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"the hypothesis's ``for a fixed $k$'' permits. For the record, SETH\nimplies ETH. Suppose 3-SAT had a $2^{o(n)}$ algorithm. The\nSparsification Lemma of \\citet{ImpagliazzoPaturiZane2001} reduces\n$k$-SAT on $n$ variables to $2^{\\varepsilon n}$ instances with\n$O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ clauses, and so, after the clause-splitting\nreduction, to 3-SAT on $O_{k,\\varepsilon}(n)$ variables. Then\n$k$-SAT would have such an algorithm too, for every fixed $k$.\"\"\"),\n(r\"\"\"$\\mathtt{count}\\ F = 0$ (lines 205--207), so it decides unsatisfiability\ndeterministically within the SETH bound above for every $\\varepsilon$\nand every $k$; and a deterministic machine is a nondeterministic one\nwhose only computation path is the deterministic run, so every\nacceptance convention for nondeterministic time is met.\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"$\\mathtt{count}\\ F = 0$ (lines 205--207). So it decides unsatisfiability\ndeterministically within the SETH bound above for every $\\varepsilon$\nand every $k$. And a deterministic machine is a nondeterministic one\nwhose only computation path is the deterministic run, so every\nacceptance convention for nondeterministic time is met.\"\"\"),\n(r\"\"\"\\paragraph{Shape (S) in detail.} For a theorem ``SETH $\\Rightarrow$ no\n$(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$'', a $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm\nfor $Y$ obtained from the verified statement would need the exponent of\nthe SAT instance to be below $k \\log_2 c$ by a constant factor, and the\nverified statement's exponent is $A\\rho(n')$ in the variable count $n'$\nof the SAT instance with $A$ unknown; no value of $A$ is on record below\n$257$, and no base $c$ is derived from the verified statement for any\n$Y$.\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"\\paragraph{Shape (S) in detail.} Take a theorem ``SETH $\\Rightarrow$ no\n$(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm for $Y$''. A $(c-\\varepsilon)^k$ algorithm\nfor $Y$ obtained from the verified statement would need the exponent of\nthe SAT instance to be below $k \\log_2 c$ by a constant factor. The\nverified statement's exponent is $A\\rho(n')$ in the variable count $n'$\nof the SAT instance, with $A$ unknown. No value of $A$ is on record below\n$257$, and no base $c$ is derived from the verified statement for any\n$Y$.\"\"\"),\n# B.6 scaling\n(r\"\"\"median at $n = 20$. The factor is one number for both curves, so their\nratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves, $2^{\\rhon - n}$: below\n1 for $n < 50$, just above 1 at $n = 50$ ($\\rho(50) = 50.14$, ratio\n$1.10$), and above 1 from there ($2.8 \\times 10^{3}$ at\n$n = 100$ and $4.0 \\times 10^{51}$ at $n = 500$). A reader who prefers\neach curve scaled to the $n = 20$ point on its own would multiply\n$2^{\\rhon}$ by a further $2^{20 - \\rho(20)} = 2^{2.79}$ and see the two\ncurves touch at $n = 20$ and the verified one above the trivial one\nfrom there on; the common factor keeps the crossing where the statement\nputs it (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a). In that scale the\nbrute-force guarantee reaches one second near $n = 33$ and the verified\nguarantee near $n = 35$; at $n = 60$ the measured CaDiCaL median is\n$3 \\times 10^{-4}$~s against $2^n$ at $1.4 \\times 10^{8}$~s and\n$2^{\\rhon}$ at $5.4 \\times 10^{8}$~s, 11.6 and 12.2 decades\nrespectively; at $n = 500$ the decision median is above the budget\n(8 of 20 finished within 60~s), against $10^{140.6}$~s and\n$10^{192.2}$~s. Regenerate:\"\"\",\nr\"\"\"median at $n = 20$. The factor is one number for both curves, so their\nratio is the ratio of the guarantees themselves, $2^{\\rhon - n}$. That ratio is below\n1 for $n < 50$, just above 1 at $n = 50$ ($\\rho(50) = 50.14$, ratio\n$1.10$), and above 1 from there ($2.8 \\times 10^{3}$ at\n$n = 100$ and $4.0 \\times 10^{51}$ at $n = 500$). A reader who prefers\neach curve scaled to the $n = 20$ point on its own would multiply\n$2^{\\rhon}$ by a further $2^{20 - \\rho(20)} = 2^{2.79}$. That reader would see the two\ncurves touch at $n = 20$ and the verified one above the trivial one\nfrom there on. The common factor keeps the crossing where the statement\nputs it (\\texttt{ETH\\_AMBIGUOUS.MD}, 06a). In that scale the\nbrute-force guarantee reaches one second near $n = 33$ and the verified\nguarantee near $n = 35$. At 