e, not in a commit message (the driver writes it into every   commit body). A question about its wording or about a claim it makes   goes to `paper3/ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD`, never into the title. The gate checks   the `\\title` against the string above. - **The paper is written in plain English** (owner, 2026-11-05, 05:30).   A reader who has never seen complexity theory must be able to follow   the thesis from the abstract to the conclusion. Concretely: short   sentences, one idea each; every symbol defined in words the first time   it appears and paraphrased in words wherever it carries the argument   (`ρ(n)`, `2^n`, \"the exponent\", \"the bound\"); no term of art without a   one-clause gloss on first use (ETH, SETH, k-CNF, kernel, comparator,   nanoda, model counter, CDCL); the abstract and the introduction carry   no formula at all beyond `2^n`; tables and figures have captions a   layperson can read; the technical detail lives in the appendix and in   the companion files, cited from the plain text. Precision is kept, not   dropped: \"the verified guarantee\", never \"the algorithm\", where that is   the fact. Every section already drafted is rewritten to this rule by   the plain-English item; every section drafted after this rule follows   it from the first line. The gate does not check prose; the refuter   item (10) does, and files every sentence a layperson could not follow. - **No stopping midway; ambiguity is decided, recorded, and passed.**   Every item makes its own choice on anything it finds ambiguous, writes   one entry per question in `paper3/ETH_AMBIGUOUS.MD` — what was ambiguous,   what the item chose, what the owner may want to change — and continues.   An item never ends on a question to the owner, never waits, never marks   itself `[!]` for want of a decision. `[!]` is for a hard failure (a build   that will not finish inside the cap, a tool that cannot be obtained), and   even then the section is drafted from what was obtained and the gap is   stated